.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt PROC 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm proc
.Nd kernel tables and counters as files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /proc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each file is generated into a buffer when opened, and every read through
that descriptor uses the same snapshot until a read from offset 0 after
the first, through
.Xr lseek 2
back to the start or
.Xr pread64 2
at 0, which generates it again, as Linux does.
A process that is gone by then makes that read fail with
.Er ESRCH .
The exceptions are
.Pa /proc/PID/oom_score_adj ,
.Pa /proc/PID/mem ,
.Pa /proc/acpi/call
and the files of
.Pa /proc/sys ,
which each read reaches as they are then.
A read or a write holds the descriptor's open file description for its
length, as
.Xr lseek 2
holds it to move the offset, so a seek through a descriptor that shares
it waits for the transfer and is not lost when the transfer ends; Linux
holds the file's position lock the same way.
A report whose text cannot be allocated is refused with
.Er ENOMEM ,
and
.Pa /proc/disk ,
the one file with a fixed buffer, is refused with
.Er EOVERFLOW
when it fills; a USB record longer than its 96-byte buffer is cut short,
its newline possibly with it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -ohang
.It Pa /proc/acpi/call
An ACPI method of the firmware evaluated by its path, as Linux's
.Ql acpi_call
module offers, for root alone: the file's mode is 0600.
A write names the method by its absolute path, such as
.Ql \e_SB.PCI0.LPCB.EC0._Q11 ,
and up to seven arguments after it, each after a space, a number in
decimal or in hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x ,
or a string in double quotes; a newline may end it.
A read gives the result of the last call and a NUL byte: an integer as
.Ql 0x
and its hexadecimal digits, a string in double quotes, a buffer as
.Ql {0x01, 0x02} ,
a package as
.Ql [A, B]
of its elements,
.Ql 0x0
for a method that returns nothing, an error as
.Ql Error:
and ACPICA's name for it, such as
.Ql Error: AE_NOT_FOUND
for a method that is not there, and
.Ql not called
before the first call.
A write that names a method takes the whole request, whatever the method
gave, as Linux's module does, and the result says how it went.
A request of 512 bytes or more is
.Er EINVAL ,
and a write while the kernel runs no ACPI interpreter is
.Er ENODEV .
.It Pa /proc/asound
Linux's ALSA files for the sound cards: card 0 the analog HD Audio
controller's, while its driver runs, and the next the card of the Intel
display's HDMI codec, which
.Pa /dev/dsp1 ,
.Pa /dev/adsp1
and
.Pa /dev/dsp2
play through, card 0 when there is no analog card; with both, card 1 is
the one they play, as Linux's OSS emulation ties them.
.Pa cards
lists each as Linux does, such as
.Ql " 0 [PCH            ]: HDA-Intel - HDA Intel PCH" ,
with the name Linux gives the controller,
.Ql "HDA Intel PCH" ,
.Ql "HDA Intel" ,
.Ql "HDA NVidia"
or
.Ql "HD-Audio Generic" ,
and a line with its address and interrupt, or reads
.Ql --- no soundcards ---
while there is none.
The directory
.Pa card Ns Ar K
of each card has
.Pa id ,
the last word of that name, such as
.Ql PCH
or
.Ql HDMI ;
the HDMI card's also has
.Pa eld# Ns Ar C . Ns Ar N
for each device entry
.Ar N
of each HDMI or DisplayPort pin of the codec at address
.Ar C ,
in the form of Linux's
.Fn snd_hdmi_print_eld_info :
.Ql monitor_present ,
.Ql eld_valid ,
the pin, entry and converter, and for a valid ELD the monitor's name,
connection type, versions, IDs, latency, speakers and each short audio
descriptor.
An ELD file is mode 0644, and root may write it, as on Linux: at the last
close, each line of a field's name and a hex value sets that field, from
.Ql monitor_present ,
.Ql eld_valid ,
.Ql connection_type ,
.Ql port_id ,
.Ql support_hdcp ,
.Ql support_ai ,
.Ql audio_sync_delay ,
.Ql speakers ,
.Ql sad_count
and
.Ql sad Ns Ar N Ns _coding_type ,
.Ql _channels ,
.Ql _rates ,
.Ql _bits ,
.Ql _max_bitrate
and
.Ql _profile ;
the monitor's name, IDs and versions are kept.
What was written changes what the file reads and what the next open of
the monitor's device allows, until the display sends the monitor's ELD again.
A write past 16 KiB is
.Er EIO .
The Sound Blaster 16 is not listed.
.It Pa /proc/audio
The active audio driver and cached HDA enumeration and stream state.
The file exists when audio is disabled; then
.Ql hda ready 0
indicates no selected HDA path.
Opening it sends no codec verb.
.Pp
The codec line identifies the codec address, vendor and audio function group.
Primary and headphone lines give pin, DAC and expanded connection indices;
the primary line also gives its optional mixer or selector.
.Ql enabled
is whether the pin plays, as the last start or jack change set it; a stop
turns both off.
The headphone line ends with
.Ql mode ,
the output routing:
.Ql auto ,
the headphones while their jack has a plug and the primary pin otherwise,
following the jack while a stream plays, or
.Ql primary ,
.Ql hp
or
.Ql both .
.Pp
Each
.Ql jack
line, one for the headphone jack and one for the microphone jack, gives
the pin watched, or 0 when there is none with presence detect; its state,
.Ql in ,
.Ql out ,
or
.Ql unknown
before a reading succeeds; and the readings of its sense, those that
failed or found the command interface busy, and the changes of its state.
The driver's thread reads each jack every quarter second, and a start
reads it once.
A state changes when two readings in a row differ from it, or at a start's
reading; a failed reading changes nothing.
A change of the microphone jack moves capture between the internal and the
jack microphone.
.Pp
On a codec with a beep widget the
.Ql beep
line gives its node, the divider of the tone it sounds, 0 when silent, 1
while a tone turned the output pins on with no stream playing, and the
tones it has begun; see
.Xr console 4 .
.Pp
The stream line gives the last successful request and programmed rate,
then controller, primary DAC and headphone DAC format words read back
at setup, and
.Ql bits ,
the bits of each sample the controller's word plays: 16, or 24 for the
mix on a codec whose DACs have 24 bits.
Before any stream these fields are zero.
Stopping clears
.Ql playing
and retains the last setup.
.Pp
Each
.Ql caps dac
line gives the DAC, the node supplying its effective capabilities, and
hexadecimal PCM and stream-format masks.
PCM bits 0 through 11 correspond, in order, to 8000, 11025, 16000, 22050,
32000, 44100, 48000, 88200, 96000, 176400, 192000 and 384000 Hz.
PCM bits 16 through 20 indicate 8, 16, 20, 24 and 32-bit samples.
Stream-format bit 0 indicates PCM.
.Pp
The selected pins and mixer have
.Ql connections nid
lines with the long-form flag and packed hexadecimal entries.
Each following
.Ql expanded
line lists NIDs in selector-index order, starting at index zero.
.Ql invalid
marks a rejected list; its raw entries stop where decoding failed.
.Pp
The
.Ql input
line gives the input path chosen at discovery, or by the mixer's record
source since: the ADC and its connection index toward the pin, a mixer or
selector between them and its index, or 0, the pin recorded, the internal
and jack microphone pins, whether the internal mic is the inverted stereo
pair whose right channel is muted, the ADC's input gain on the pin recorded
in steps, as
.Xr mixer 4
last set it, and the pin widget control the last capture set.
The
.Ql caps adc
line gives the ADC's capabilities as a
.Ql caps dac
line does a DAC's.
The
.Ql capture
line gives the input stream: whether it runs, the rate asked for and the
one programmed, the format word read back, the bytes of one DMA period and
the periods in the buffer, the frames handed to the reader, the overruns,
services that found a period overwritten before it was handed over, and the
position in the DMA buffer at the last service.
The counters start again at each start.
.Pp
The
.Ql queue
line is read when the file is opened, from memory and the stream's
registers.
.Ql fill
and
.Ql high
are the bytes in the write ring and the most it has held;
.Ql delay-ms
is the time a sample written now waits to be played, the ring at the
requested rate and the DMA buffer ahead of the position at the programmed
one;
.Ql written
and
.Ql dropped
count the bytes the ring took, and those it refused or emptied after a
missed revolution;
.Ql periods
counts the DMA periods filled,
.Ql underruns
those the ring could not fill whole, and
.Ql missed
the whole revolutions of the DMA buffer that passed between two refills.
These start again at each stream's start.
.Ql lpib
is the position in the DMA buffer, in bytes.
.Pp
The
.Ql service
line spans stream starts and stops.
.Ql calls
counts khda service calls modulo 2^32;
.Ql usec
and
.Ql max-usec
are the sum and maximum of time measured around those services by the
TSC, including nested interrupts, in 64-bit microseconds.
They exclude the thread's wait to run and do not use its tick accounting.
They are zero without a calibrated TSC.
.Ql missed
counts playback revolutions missed since driver initialization, modulo
2^32, including those before khda starts.
The remaining fields describe the latest miss, or are zero before one:
.Ql clock-us
is the TSC timestamp in microseconds, on the same clock as debugfs's
.Pa bkl/long ,
not uptime;
.Ql gap-us
is the WALCLK interval since the preceding playback service, or since the
stream's start for its first service;
.Ql asked-us
is the interval since the first pending playback request;
.Ql asked-valid
is 1 when that request belongs to this stream start, otherwise 0;
.Ql moved-us
is the position's modular movement converted to microseconds; and
.Ql revolution-us
is the duration of the DMA buffer.
The WALCLK intervals wrap after about 179 seconds.
The timestamp is zero without a calibrated TSC.
.Pp
The
.Ql gpio
line gives the function group's GPIO count and its whole GPIO Count
parameter.
Each
.Ql widget
line is one widget of the function group, read at discovery: its NID, its
type
.Po Ql out ,
.Ql in ,
.Ql mix ,
.Ql sel ,
.Ql pin ,
.Ql power ,
.Ql volume ,
.Ql beep
or
.Ql vendor
.Pc ,
its audio widget capabilities, and its input and output amplifier
capabilities, the widget's own under Amp Param Override and the function
group's otherwise, or zero where it has no such amplifier.
A pin adds its pin capabilities and configuration default, and a pin with
presence detect its Get Pin Sense word, read at the last stream's start
when the read succeeded, whose bit 31 reports a plug.
.Ql conn
lists the widget's connections, expanded, in selector order;
.Ql conn invalid
marks a list that is malformed or longer than 32.
The
.Ql adcs
line lists the audio input widgets.
These are enumeration and setup records, not a measurement of sound.
Use
.Xr audiotest 1
to submit a tone from SSH.
.It Pa /proc/bus/input/devices
A record for each input device that
.Pa /sys/class/input
lists, in the order of its
.Pa input Ns Ar N ,
as Linux's input core writes it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
I: Bus=0011 Vendor=0002 Product=0007 Version=01b1
N: Name="SynPS/2 Synaptics TouchPad"
P: Phys=isa0060/serio1/input0
S: Sysfs=/devices/platform/i8042/serio1/input/input5
U: Uniq=
H: Handlers=event5
B: PROP=1
B: EV=b
B: KEY=e520 30000 0 0 0 0
B: ABS=660800011000003
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ql I:
line has the device's IDs in four hexadecimal digits each,
.Ql N: ,
.Ql P:
and
.Ql U:
its name, place and serial,
.Ql S:
its directory below
.Pa /sys ,
and
.Ql H:
the name of each handler's node, each followed by a space.
The one handler is evdev's
.Pa event Ns Ar N ;
Rach2 has no
.Ql kbd ,
.Ql sysrq ,
.Ql mouse Ns Ar N
or
.Ql js Ns Ar N
handler to list beside it.
A
.Ql B:
line gives a mask of the device's properties, its event types, and the
codes of each type it has, in that order, as
.Pa /sys/class/input/input Ns Ar N Ns Pa /capabilities
prints them: the kernel's longs in hexadecimal from the highest that has a
bit, and 32-bit words to an i386 program on x86-64.
A blank line ends each record.
.It Pa /proc/bus/input/handlers
The input handlers, as Linux lists them:
.Ql "N: Number=0 Name=evdev Minor=64" .
.It Pa /proc/cmdline
The boot command line, followed by a newline.
.It Pa /proc/cpuinfo
The processor, in Linux's format for x86, from what
.Ql cpuid
reports:
.Li vendor_id ,
.Li cpu family ,
.Li model ,
.Li model name ,
.Li stepping ,
.Li cache size ,
the last-level cache's,
.Li apicid ,
.Li cpuid level ,
.Li flags ,
the feature bits by Linux's names, among them
.Li pku
where the processor has protection keys,
.Li ospke
where the kernel turned them on, and
.Li xsaves
except on an AMD Zen 1 or Zen 2 without the microcode that fixes its
Erratum 1386, as Linux,
.Li clflush size
and
.Li address sizes ,
each a
.Ql name<TAB>: value
line, and a blank line after the record.
There is a record for each processor started, with its number in
.Li processor
and its local APIC's in
.Li apicid ;
the processors are taken to be alike, so
.Ql cpuid
on the running one answers for the others.
.Li physical id
and
.Li core id
are the processor's package and its core in the package, which its APIC ID
holds at the widths
.Ql cpuid
gives;
.Li siblings
counts the processors started in the package, and
.Li cpu cores
its cores that have one.
It has no
.Li cpu MHz
or
.Li bogomips
line, since the kernel does not measure the processor's clock.
.It Pa /proc/console
Serial console and speaker counters, one
.Ql name value
pair per line:
.Bl -tag -width reader-drains
.It Li uart-bytes , uart-overruns
Bytes received by the UART and UART overruns.
.It Li irq-drains , irq-bytes
Receive drains from the interrupt handler and bytes received there.
.It Li tick-drains , reader-drains
Receive drains from timer ticks and console readers.
.It Li ring-full , ring-max
Full receive ring count and maximum ring occupancy.
.It Li shell-bytes
Bytes taken by the kernel's console polls.
.It Li line-drops , queue-drops
Drops from the input line and input queue.
.It Li flush-bytes , partial-moved , read-bytes
Bytes discarded by a flush, moved from a partial canonical line to
raw input, and read.
.It Li queue-max , line-max
Maximum input queue and line occupancy.
.It Li speaker-tones , speaker-sounds
Tones begun by
.Dv KDMKTONE
and sounds begun by
.Dv KIOCSOUND ;
see
.Xr ioctl 2 .
.It Li speaker-ends , speaker-cuts
Tones the kernel ended at their length, and tones and sounds a later
request ended or replaced.
.It Li speaker-divisor , speaker-ms
The last tone's or sound's divisor of 1193182 Hz, and the last tone's
length in milliseconds, 0 for a sound.
.It Li speaker-beeps , speaker-beep-divider
Tones and sounds the audio driver's beep generator began beside the PC
speaker, and the divider it was last given, 0 for silence; both stay 0 on
a machine whose driver has no generator.
While the driver's thread runs, a request or the kernel's stop hands the
divider to that thread, and this line has it as soon as it is handed on,
before the codec has it; the count is then of tones handed to the thread,
sent or replaced by a later request before the thread sent them, where
.Pa /proc/audio
counts the tones the codec was sent.
.It Li speaker-beep-retries
Stops of the generator that the tick found the codec busy for and sent
again at a later tick; 0 while the driver's thread sends the generator's
commands.
.El
.It Pa /proc/diskstats
A line for each disk and each partition node, in Linux's twenty fields:
the device's major and minor numbers and its name, then the reads that
ended well, 0 merged, their sectors and their milliseconds, the same four
of the writes, the requests in progress, the milliseconds the device was
busy and the sum of the transfers' milliseconds, and six zeros, for the
discards and the flushes, which are not counted per device.
A disk's line counts every transfer to it, through any of its nodes, and
has its line whether or not its node is published; a partition's counts
its node's transfers and those of the boot root mounted on it.
BusyBox's
.Xr iostat 1
reads it.
.It Pa /proc/partitions
The header Linux prints and a blank line, then a line for each disk and
partition
.Pa /proc/diskstats
lists: the major and minor numbers, the size in kibibytes and the name,
in Linux's columns.
BusyBox's
.Xr fdisk 8
reads it to find the disks
.Fl l
lists.
.It Pa /proc/vmstat
Linux's names and values of the counters the kernel keeps: nr_free_pages,
nr_file_pages, the pages of the page and buffer caches, pgpgin and pgpgout,
the kibibytes read from and written to the whole disks since boot,
nr_anon_transparent_hugepages, the transparent huge pages mapped, each
once however many processes map it, the huge zero page aside, and pswpin and pswpout, 0, since nothing is
swapped; then the events of transparent huge pages the kernel counts:
thp_fault_alloc, thp_fault_fallback, thp_collapse_alloc,
thp_collapse_alloc_failed, thp_split_page, thp_underused_split_page,
thp_split_pmd, thp_zero_page_alloc and thp_zero_page_alloc_failed.
procps-ng's
.Xr vmstat 8
reads it, and takes a counter it does not find as 0.
.It Pa /proc/locks
A line for each advisory lock held, the newest first, in Linux's form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
1: POSIX  ADVISORY  WRITE PID MAJ:MIN:INODE START END
.Ed
.Pp
The kind is
.Ql POSIX
for a record lock of
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Ql OFDLCK
for an open file description's, whose PID is \-1, and
.Ql FLOCK
for a lock of
.Xr flock 2 ,
whose range is
.Ql 0 EOF .
The type is
.Ql WRITE
or
.Ql READ ;
MAJ and MIN are the device numbers of the file's mount in hexadecimal and
INODE its inode number, as
.Xr stat 2
gives them; END is
.Ql EOF
for a lock to the end of the file.
A wait for a lock has no line.
util-linux's
.Xr lslocks 8
reads it.
.It Pa /proc/lockdep
In the kernel built with the lock-order checker,
.Ql make rach2-lockdep ,
alone: each lock class the checker has seen, in Linux's form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
all lock classes:
0xKEY FD: 2 BD: 1 +.: NAME
 -> NAME2
.Ed
.Pp
KEY is the address that keys the class, the lock's own for a static lock
and its init site's for a lock in an allocated record;
FD and BD count the orders from and to it;
.Ql +
in the first mark says it was taken in an interrupt and
.Ql \-
in the second that it was held with interrupts on;
NAME is the lock's name, with
.Ql / Ns Ar N
after it for subclass N.
A line
.Ql -> NAME2
follows for each class taken while it was held.
The text stops at 128 KiB.
.It Pa /proc/lockdep_stats
In the same kernel alone: the classes, orders and chains the checker holds
against the size of each pool, the reports made and those not made because
none more could be kept, and
.Ql checking: on ,
or
.Ql checking: off:
and the pool whose filling stopped the checker.
.It Pa /proc/swaps
Linux's header line and no line under it, since the kernel swaps nothing
and has no
.Xr swapon 2 ,
which answers
.Er ENOSYS ;
util-linux's
.Xr swapon 8
lists the areas from it.
.It Pa /proc/slabinfo
Linux's version 2.1 header and one line for each size class of the kernel
heap, kmalloc-16 to kmalloc-2048 by doubling, as procps-ng's
.Xr slabtop 1
reads it.
A slab is one page of the class: its objects are the blocks the page holds
after a 16-byte header, the active objects those in use, and the tunables
and shared counts are 0.
Objects larger than 2048 bytes take whole pages and are not listed.
Every user may read it, where Linux's is readable by root alone.
.It Pa /proc/disk
Disk records and counters, described in
.Xr disk 7 .
The initial record has named fields for disk 0, its probe state,
driver policy, identity, size, partitions, and counters.
Before a probe it reports
.Ql state not-asked ;
a probe that finds no disk reports
.Ql state no-device .
.Pp
The registered disks follow in this form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
disk N TRANSPORT LOCATION SECTORS TABLE MODEL
identity N SECTOR-SIZE fixed|removable mounted|free SERIAL
part N M START COUNT ID LABEL
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar N
is the disk number and
.Ar M
the partition number.
.Ar START
and
.Ar COUNT
are sectors.
.Ar TRANSPORT
is
.Ql ahci ,
.Ql ata ,
.Ql nvme ,
.Ql usb ,
or
.Ql virtio .
.Ar LOCATION
is the AHCI port; the ATA position; the NVMe controller's number times
four plus the namespace's place among those the controller published;
the USB class-driver slot times four plus the logical unit number; or the
virtio block device's place among those found.
ATA positions 0 through 3 are primary master, primary slave,
secondary master, and secondary slave.
.Ar TABLE
is
.Ql mbr ,
.Ql gpt ,
.Ql gpt-bad ,
.Ql none ,
or
.Ql unread .
.Ar ID
is a GPT partition GUID or
.Ql mbr:
followed by the two-digit hexadecimal partition type.
.Ar LABEL
is the GPT name and is empty for MBR.
.Pp
Opening the file issues no disk command.
A report exceeding its fixed buffer bound is refused.
.It Pa /proc/filesystems
One file system type per line.
The two synthetic file systems have the lines
.Ql nodev devfs
and
.Ql nodev proc ,
with a tab between fields.
Each remaining line is a type accepted for mounting after a tab, and
after
.Ql nodev
for a type that stands on no device:
.Ql nodev tmpfs ,
.Ql nodev mqueue
and
.Ql nodev debugfs .
.It Pa /proc/fs/lockwait
One line for each kind of sleeping lock the kernel has used, in the order
of first use:
.Dl KIND HOLDS LONGEST_US PID NAME
A hold counts when
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/lock_stat
was nonzero at its take and its holder finished a wait for the disk while
it held the lock; it is 0 at boot, and a take then reads no clock.
HOLDS is the number of such holds since boot, modulo 2^32; LONGEST_US is
the longest of them in microseconds, which reads short past 71 minutes;
PID and NAME are that hold's thread ID and name, or
.Ql 0
and
.Ql -
while HOLDS is 0.
The kind
.Ql mount
is the namespace lock of each mounted volume.
The kind
.Ql rename
is the lock a volume whose type locks each file takes around a rename
between two directories.
A test that slows the disk reads it to see that no lock was held across
another file's request.
.It Pa /proc/interrupts
A heading names each online processor, followed by one line per device
vector, numbered 32 through 239 except 128, with a nonzero interrupt count:
.Dl VECTOR: CPU0_COUNT CPU1_COUNT ... SOURCE NAMES
Each count belongs to the processor named in its heading column.
The source is
.Ql PIC
for a line the 8259 pair delivers,
.Ql IO-APIC
for a line the I/O APIC delivers,
.Ql LAPIC
for the tick of the local APIC's timer, and
.Ql MSI
for a device's message vector.
Fields are separated by tabs.
Shared handlers have comma-separated names; an unnamed vector has
.Ql none .
The final lines are
.Ql SPU: COUNT spurious
and
.Ql FBK: COUNT usb fallback ,
for APIC spurious interrupts and USB timer drains that found work without
an observed pending USB interrupt after any USB interrupt source was enabled.
The USB pump checks each configured controller's MSI vector or PCI IRQ.
A message still in transit can also increase the fallback count.
.It Pa /proc/irq/N/smp_affinity
The requested processor mask of enabled PCI message vector N, in hexadecimal,
with a comma between 32-bit words, the highest word first.
A privileged write moves the message to the least allocated reachable online
processor in the mask.
A zero mask restores the default of all reachable online processors.
A mask with no reachable online processor fails with EINVAL.
Before the other processors start, messages go to the boot processor;
afterwards they are spread by the number allocated to each processor.
The requested mask is retained when its last online processor departs and
placement falls back to the remaining processors.
There is no runtime processor-hotplug interface.
The directory and its files disappear when the message is disabled.
.It Pa /proc/irq/N/effective_affinity
The processor actually chosen for message vector N, in the same hexadecimal
format as smp_affinity.
This file is read-only.
A message already sent to the old processor before migration still reaches
the same vector's handler.
.It Pa /proc/loadavg
One line, as Linux writes it: the load averages over one, five and fifteen
minutes with two decimals, the processes running or ready to run and all
the processes, threads counted, separated by a slash, and the last
process ID given out.
The averages are Linux's: every five seconds and a tick the processes
running or ready to run are counted, and each average decays toward the
count by the share of its minute span that five seconds is.
Linux counts the processes in an uninterruptible wait as well, which this
kernel does not tell apart from the other sleepers.
.It Pa /proc/loop
One line per occupied loop device:
.Dl loopN BYTES rw|ro writes WRITES syncs SYNCS PATH
The fields give its size in bytes, write permission, write and sync
counts, and the first 63 bytes of the path used for attachment, or
.Ql (gone)
for a device whose medium was removed while a mount or a transfer still
holds it.
An unused device has no line.
.It Pa /proc/meminfo
Named memory counts, one
.Ql name: value
pair per line.
.Li MemTotal
and
.Li MemFree
give the total and free physical frame pool: the usable memory below the
architecture's reach less what the boot reserved, which is the memory below
4 GiB on i686 and all of the memory on x86-64, up to 64 TiB, or 64 GiB on a
processor without 1 GiB pages.
.Li Buffers
and
.Li Cached
give resident buffer-cache memory and the memory of the page caches: the
disk window's, and the file page cache's, the pages of ext2 files that
.Xr read 2 ,
exec and private mappings read, kept while memory is free.
.Li MemAvailable
is free memory plus both caches and
.Li KReclaimable .
.Li SwapCached
is
.Ql 0 kB ,
since nothing is swapped.
These values have the suffix
.Ql kB
and are in kibibytes.
.Li HeapPages
is the kernel heap's page count;
.Li HeapUsed
is the number of bytes in use within it.
.Li LowTotal
and
.Li LowFree
give the total and free frames of the low window, the first 64 MiB, whose
addresses every device can be given: the pool of the kernel's frames for
device buffers, the disk window's cache and the virtio queues, taken by user
pages only once the memory above it is gone, as Linux's low memory is.
.Li SwapTotal
and
.Li SwapFree
are both
.Ql 0 kB .
.Li AnonHugePages
is the memory transparent huge pages map, 2 MiB for each, however many
processes map it, the huge zero page aside.
.Li KReclaimable
is the kernel heap's freed runs, kept mapped for reuse, which reclaim gives
back.
.Li Slab ,
the last line, is the kernel heap's pages but the buffer cache's, which
the heap holds, in kibibytes; BusyBox's nmeter reads it to count the memory
in use.
.It Pa /proc/mounts
One line for each mount the reader's mount namespace sees, with six
whitespace-separated fields:
.Dl SOURCE TARGET TYPE OPTIONS 0 0
The root, the synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
mounts, the tmpfs at
.Pa /dev/shm ,
the synthetic
.Pa /sys ,
read-only, the
.Ql mqueue
mount at
.Pa /dev/mqueue
and the
.Ql debugfs
mount at
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug
precede mounts made by programs, which follow in the order they were made,
so that a mount over another's root comes after it.
A mount outside the reader's root is left out, and the target is the
mount's path from the reader's root, as after
.Xr pivot_root 2 .
The source, target, type, and options describe the mounted file system;
a bind mount has the source of the mount it shows.
Options are comma-separated and read from the current flags, so that a
remount shows at once:
.Ql ro
when the mount or its file system is read-only, otherwise
.Ql rw ;
the file system's
.Ql sync
and
.Ql dirsync ;
the mount's own
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev ,
.Ql noexec ,
.Ql noatime ,
.Ql nodiratime
and
.Ql relatime ;
and last the file system's other options, such as
.Ql window
for a KRFFS root read through the page-cache window, or
.Ql hidepid=invisible
for a proc mount that hides the processes its reader may not trace; see
.Xr mount 2 .
The root,
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
are mounted
.Ql relatime ,
as Linux mounts by default, and a remount that names no atime flag keeps
the mount's.
.It Pa /proc/net/arp
A header followed by one row per complete ARP cache entry.
The columns are IP address, hardware type, flags, hardware address,
mask, and device.
IP addresses are dotted decimal, padded to 16 characters; hardware
addresses are six colon-separated bytes in lowercase hexadecimal, as
Linux writes them.
Hardware type is
.Ql 0x1 ,
flags are
.Ql 0x2 ,
or
.Ql 0x6
for a mapping set by hand as permanent, and the mask is
.Ql * .
The device is the interface on which the mapping was learned.
.It Pa /proc/net/softnet_stat
One line for each processor online, of fifteen eight-digit hexadecimal
fields, as Linux 6.12 writes it: the datagrams the processor's drains of
the loopback backlog delivered, those it dropped because more than
.Pa /proc/sys/net/core/netdev_max_backlog
datagrams waited on its input queue, and its drains that ended on
.Pa /proc/sys/net/core/netdev_budget
or
.Pa /proc/sys/net/core/netdev_budget_usecs ;
then zeros in the six fields Linux no longer counts and in
the counts of RPS and the flow limit; the backlog's length; the
processor's number; and the backlog's length again as its input queue,
which no drain has taken yet, and its process queue, which a drain has
taken and not yet delivered.
The backlog is the machine's, so its lengths are shown on the boot
processor's line alone.
.It Pa /proc/net/wireless
Wireless station quality, signal, noise and discard counters; see
.Xr wlan 4 .
.It Pa /proc/net/dev
Two header lines followed by one line per network interface.
Each line begins with the interface name and a colon, then sixteen
counters in this order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Receive:  bytes packets errs drop fifo frame compressed multicast
Transmit: bytes packets errs drop fifo colls carrier compressed
.Ed
.Pp
The interface's bytes, packets, receive errors and drops are 64-bit
counts, which do not wrap in practice.
The receive FIFO and both compressed counters are zero.
Receive drops include frames missed by the card.
The frame, multicast, transmit error, FIFO, collision, and carrier
counts come from the card when its driver supplies them; otherwise
they are zero.
.It Pa /proc/net/if_inet6
One line for each IPv6 address of each interface, with no header, as
Linux writes it and BusyBox's
.Ic ifconfig
reads it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fe80000000000000505400fffe123456 01 40 20 80     eth0
.Ed
.Pp
The address in 32 hexadecimal digits, the interface's index, the prefix
length, the scope
.Ql ( 00
global,
.Ql 10
host,
.Ql 20
link,
.Ql 40
site) and Linux's
.Dv IFA_F_
flags, each two hexadecimal digits, and the interface's name, right
aligned in eight columns.
.It Pa /proc/net/ipv6_route
One line for each route of IPv6's table, with no header: the
destination and its prefix length, the source and its, always :: and 0,
the next hop, :: on the link, then the metric, reference count, use and
flags in eight hexadecimal digits each, and the interface's name.
The flags are Linux's:
.Ql 00000001
up,
.Ql 00000002
through a gateway,
.Ql 00010000
a router's default route,
.Ql 00040000
made from a router's advertisement and
.Ql 00400000
with a lifetime that ends.
.Ic route -A inet6
prints this file.
.It Pa /proc/net/route
A header followed by each interface's default route, when it has one,
and the subnets of its addresses, then the routes programs added.
The eleven columns are
.Ql Iface Destination Gateway Flags RefCnt Use Metric Mask MTU Window IRTT .
Addresses and masks are eight hexadecimal digits in reversed byte order.
Flags are hexadecimal:
.Ql 0001
for a route on the link and
.Ql 0003
for one through a gateway.
The metric is the route's; reference count, use, MTU, window, and IRTT are
zero.
.It Pa /proc/net/stat/rt_cache , /proc/net/stat/arp_cache
Linux's heading and one line of hexadecimal counters, which
.Ic lnstat
reads.
Of the route counters,
.Ql entries
is the routes of the table,
.Ql in_slow_tot
the datagrams received,
.Ql in_no_route
those for another host and
.Ql out_slow_tot
the lookups of the output path; the others are zero.
Of the neighbour counters,
.Ql entries
is the mappings held,
.Ql allocs
and
.Ql destroys
those stored and dropped,
.Ql lookups
and
.Ql hits
the lookups and the ones that found a mapping,
.Ql res_failed
the resolutions that got no answer,
.Ql unresolved_discards
the datagrams dropped waiting for one, and
.Ql table_fulls
the mappings refused for a cache full of permanent ones.
There is no
.Pa nf_conntrack ,
since the kernel tracks no connections, so
.Ic ctstat
finds nothing to read.
.It Pa /proc/net/tcp
A header followed by rows for the TCP connections of
.Dv AF_INET
sockets, including listeners and connections still closing.
The kernel counts connections, allocates the snapshot array, then copies
up to the original count.
Connections created during allocation may be included and can displace
older connections from the snapshot.
Each row gives a decimal index followed by a colon, local and remote
addresses, state, transmit and receive queue sizes, timers,
retransmission count, user ID, timeout, and inode.
.Pp
Addresses have the form
.Ql AAAAAAAA:PPPP :
an eight-digit hexadecimal IPv4 address in reversed byte order and a
four-digit hexadecimal port.
A listener's local address is the one it bound, 0.0.0.0 for one bound
to
.Dv INADDR_ANY ,
as on Linux.
State is the two-digit hexadecimal Linux state number.
Queue sizes are paired as
.Ql TTTTTTTT:RRRRRRRR ;
a listener's receive queue is the connections waiting to be accepted, as
Linux gives it.
The timer pair is
.Ql 00:00000000 ;
the retransmission count has eight hexadecimal digits.
User ID and inode are decimal; timeout is zero.
User ID and inode remain available while the connection is closing.
.Pp
After the inode, each row has the fixed fields
.Ql 1 0000000000000000 100 0 0 10 0
and decimal values with the prefixes
.Ql wnd= ,
.Ql cwnd= ,
.Ql flight= ,
.Ql unsent= ,
.Ql rto= ,
.Ql zwp= ,
.Ql refused= ,
and
.Ql for= .
.It Pa /proc/net/udp , /proc/net/raw
A header followed by a row for each UDP or raw socket, in the columns of
.Pa /proc/net/tcp
up to the inode, which are followed by the reference count 2, a pointer of
zeros and a drop count of 0.
The local address is the one the socket bound, 0.0.0.0 for any and for a
raw socket; a raw socket's port is its protocol, 255 for the descriptor that
takes only configuration ioctls.
The state is 01 for a connected socket and 07 for the rest, as Linux writes
them, and only the receive queue is counted.
.It Pa /proc/net/tcp6 , /proc/net/udp6 , /proc/net/raw6
The
.Dv AF_INET6
sockets, in the columns of
.Pa /proc/net/tcp ,
.Pa /proc/net/udp
and
.Pa /proc/net/raw ,
of which the IPv4 files list none.
An address is 32 hexadecimal digits, its four 32-bit words each in reversed
byte order, as Linux writes them: ::1 is
.Ql 00000000000000000000000001000000 .
An IPv4 peer of an
.Dv AF_INET6
socket is written as its mapped address, ::ffff:a.b.c.d, and any address
as ::.
.It Pa /proc/net/unix
A header followed by a row for each
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket with a descriptor open: its address, which is zeros, the reference
count, the protocol 0 and the flags, eight hexadecimal digits each, 00010000
for a listener; the type, four; the state, 03 for a connected socket and 01
for the rest; the inode in decimal; and the name, if it has one, an abstract
name written with
.Ql @
for its leading NUL byte.
.It Pa /proc/net/packet
Linux's heading and a row for each bound
.Dv AF_PACKET
socket, which
.Ql ss -0
reads: the socket's address, which is zeros as wide as a pointer; the
reference count; the type, 2 for
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM ;
the protocol, a four-digit hexadecimal EtherType or 0000; the interface
index, 0 for every interface; 1 when the socket has a protocol and is
offered frames, else 0; the bytes queued to be read; the owner's user ID;
and the inode.
Linux also lists a packet socket that is not bound; here a socket has a row
only from its bind to its close, the time it is in the table the receive
path delivers from.
.It Pa /proc/net/sockstat
The sockets in use, the lines Linux has:
.Ql sockets: used ,
every socket of every family;
.Ql TCP: inuse orphan tw alloc mem ,
the connections in a state other than CLOSED and TIME_WAIT, those of them
with no socket left, those in TIME_WAIT, every one not in TIME_WAIT, and the
pages their control blocks and receive rings take;
.Ql UDP: inuse mem ,
the UDP sockets and the pages of datagrams waiting to be read;
.Ql UDPLITE: inuse ,
.Ql RAW: inuse
and
.Ql FRAG: inuse memory ,
which is always zero, since a fragment is dropped on arrival.
.It Pa /proc/net/snmp
Of the MIB groups Linux has, a line of names and a line of values for the
fields this kernel keeps, in Linux's order, which
.Xr nstat 8
reads by name.
.Ql Ip:
has Forwarding, 2, not a router, DefaultTTL, 64, and the datagrams
received, dropped for a bad header, for another host's address, for an
unknown protocol and as broadcasts other than UDP or for want of memory,
delivered, sent, refused by a full card and sent to no route; then
ReasmTimeout, ReasmReqds, ReasmOKs and ReasmFails, the reassemblies that
ran out of time, the fragments received, the datagrams reassembled and
those given up, and FragOKs, FragFails and FragCreates, the datagrams sent
in fragments, those refused or cut short, and the fragments made.
.Ql Icmp:
counts the messages received and the short ones, the destination
unreachables, the echo requests and replies each way, and the messages
sent.
.Ql Tcp:
has RtoAlgorithm 1, the retransmission bounds in milliseconds, MaxConn -1
for no fixed limit, the connects and accepted handshakes, the handshakes and
open connections a reset or a timeout ended, CurrEstab, the connections
ESTABLISHED or in CLOSE_WAIT, and the segments received, sent, retransmitted
and dropped as malformed or as a SYN in ESTABLISHED, and the resets sent.
.Ql Udp:
counts the IPv4 datagrams delivered, those for a port no socket has, the
malformed ones, those with a bad checksum and those a full queue dropped
among them, and those sent, then RcvbufErrors, the datagrams a receive
queue's budget dropped.
A broadcast for a port no socket has counts as NoPorts, where Linux counts
it in a field of its own, IgnoredMulti.
The counters start at zero at boot and wrap at 2^32.
.It Pa /proc/net/netstat
Of the
.Ql TcpExt:
counters Linux has, a line of names and a line of values for those this
kernel keeps, in Linux's order, which
.Xr nstat 8
reads by name: DelayedACKLost, the segments of data received again whole;
TCPTimeouts, the retransmission timeouts; TCPAbortOnData, the
connections ended by data no program will read, past the peer's FIN or
after this side's close; TCPAbortOnClose, those a close with data unread
ended; TCPAbortOnTimeout, those a timeout ended; TCPTimeWaitOverflow, those
that skipped TIME_WAIT for a full table of them; TCPChallengeACK and TCPSYNChallenge, the
challenge acknowledgements sent and the SYNs that drew one;
TCPACKSkippedSynRecv, TCPACKSkippedSeq and TCPACKSkippedChallenge, the
answers of a handshake not yet ended, those to segments outside the
window and the challenges the rate limit held back;
TCPKeepAlive, the keepalive probes sent; and TCPMTUPFail and
TCPMTUPSuccess, the probes of a larger segment lost and taken, as
.Xr tcp 7
describes them.
The counters start at zero at boot and wrap at 2^32.
.It Pa /proc/net/snmp6
A line for each IPv6, ICMPv6 and UDP counter this kernel keeps, its name in
32 columns, a tab and its value, in Linux's order, which
.Xr nstat 8
reads by name: the datagrams received, dropped for a bad header, for
another host's address and for an unknown protocol, dropped for want of
memory, delivered, forwarded, always 0, sent, refused by a full card and
sent to no route; the reassemblies timed out, the fragments received, the
datagrams reassembled and given up, the datagrams sent in fragments and
those of which a fragment could not leave, and the fragments made; the
multicast datagrams each way; and the ICMPv6 messages received, the
malformed ones, the messages sent, and those received and sent by type,
neighbour discovery's and MLD's among them, those only a router sends
always 0; and the UDP datagrams over IPv6 delivered, for a port no socket
has, malformed and sent, which
.Ql Udp:
of
.Pa /proc/net/snmp
leaves out.
The counters start at zero at boot and wrap at 2^32.
.It Pa /proc/sysvipc/shm , /proc/sysvipc/sem , /proc/sysvipc/msg
A heading and a line for each System V segment, set or queue, in Linux's
columns: the key and the id, the permission bits in octal, a segment's
size, creator, last operator and attachments, a set's semaphores, or a
queue's bytes, messages, last sender and last receiver, the four ids of
the owner and the maker, and the times in seconds since the epoch, 0 for
never; a segment's resident bytes are its size and its swapped bytes 0.
A segment of more than 4294967295 bytes, which x86-64 allows, shows that
number as its size.
See
.Xr shmget 2 ,
.Xr semget 2
and
.Xr msgget 2 .
.It Pa /proc/sys
A few of Linux's sysctl files, each a value and a newline, read-only but
for those this entry says a write sets:
.Pa fs/inotify/max_queued_events ,
16384,
.Pa fs/inotify/max_user_instances ,
128, and
.Pa fs/inotify/max_user_watches ,
8192, the limits of
.Xr inotify_init 2 ,
which a write sets for the next instance, watch or event; a value below
what a user or a queue holds takes nothing away;
.Pa fs/nr_open ,
1048576, the most descriptors a process may be allowed, the bound of a
hard
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
that
.Xr prlimit64 2
checks, which a write from the bits of a long, 32 on i386 and 64 on
x86-64, to 16777216 sets;
.Pa fs/file-max ,
the open files the machine may have before an open by a caller without
privilege fails with
.Er ENFILE ,
LONG_MAX, 2147483647 on i386 and 9223372036854775807 on x86-64, which a
write up to that sets;
.Pa fs/file-nr ,
the open files, 0 and
.Pa fs/file-max ,
separated by tabs;
.Pa fs/pipe-max-size ,
1048576, the largest capacity
.Dv F_SETPIPE_SZ
of
.Xr fcntl 2
gives a caller without privilege and the most a new pipe of one has,
which a write of a number up to 2147483648 sets, rounded up to a power of
two of 4096 or more, as Linux rounds it;
.Pa fs/pipe-user-pages-soft ,
16384, the pages the pipes of one real user may hold, each pipe's whole
capacity counted, before a new pipe of a caller without privilege gets
8192 bytes and
.Dv F_SETPIPE_SZ
refuses it a growth with
.Er EPERM ;
.Pa fs/pipe-user-pages-hard ,
0, the pages past which such a caller's new pipe fails and its growth is
refused, none while 0;
both take any unsigned long written with no sign, as on Linux;
.Pa fs/mqueue/msg_default
and
.Pa msg_max ,
10, and
.Pa msgsize_default
and
.Pa msgsize_max ,
8192, the queue
.Xr mq_open 2
makes without attributes and an unprivileged caller's largest, and
.Pa queues_max ,
256, the queues such a caller may make;
.Pa fs/protected_symlinks ,
1, under which a symbolic link at the end of a path in a sticky directory
others may write is followed only by its owner or when the directory's
owner owns it, else
.Er EACCES ;
.Pa fs/protected_hardlinks ,
1, under which a hard link to a file of another user, without
.Dv CAP_FOWNER ,
needs a regular file with no set-user-ID bit, no set-group-ID bit with
group execute, and read and write permission for the caller, else
.Er EPERM ;
.Pa fs/protected_regular ,
2, and
.Pa fs/protected_fifos ,
1, under which an
.Dv O_CREAT
open of an existing regular file or FIFO in a sticky directory, owned by
neither the caller nor the directory's owner, is
.Er EACCES
when others may write the directory, or at 2 when its group may; any other
kind of file there is refused so whatever their values;
each guards root as well, and a write of 0 turns its rule off; the first
two take 0 or 1, the others 0 to 2, at the values Debian and Ubuntu set,
where Linux's own are 0;
.Pa kernel/msgmax ,
8192,
.Pa kernel/msgmnb ,
16384,
.Pa kernel/msgmni ,
64,
.Pa kernel/sem ,
the most semaphores of a set, of every set, of one operation call and
the most sets,
.Ql 32000 2048000 500 64 ,
.Pa kernel/shmall
and
.Pa kernel/shmmax ,
4278190079 on i386 and 18446744073692774399 on x86-64, and
.Pa kernel/shmmni ,
64, the limits of System V IPC, which util-linux's
.Ic ipcs
reads;
.Pa kernel/ostype ,
.Ql Linux ;
.Pa kernel/osrelease ,
the release
.Xr uname 2
reports;
.Pa kernel/pid_max ,
2000000000 at boot, the bound below which every PID namespace gives out
process IDs; past it they start again from 300, or from 1 before 300 has
been given, and a fork finds none free fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
A privileged write sets it from 301 to 2000000000;
.Pa kernel/ns_last_pid ,
mode 0666, the last process ID the reader's PID namespace gave out, read
at each read in the namespace of the process that reads; a
write of a number from 0 to pid_max, which needs
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
or
.Dv CAP_CHECKPOINT_RESTORE
in the user namespace that owns the PID namespace, makes its next process
take the first free ID above it;
.Pa kernel/core_pattern ,
.Ql core ,
the name a core file always has here, as
.Xr core 5
says;
the lockup detectors' settings, which a write within each one's range
sets:
.Pa kernel/watchdog ,
.Pa kernel/soft_watchdog
and
.Pa kernel/nmi_watchdog ,
1,
.Pa kernel/watchdog_thresh ,
10,
.Pa kernel/softlockup_panic ,
.Pa kernel/hardlockup_panic ,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_panic
and the three
.Pa kernel/*_all_cpu_backtrace ,
0,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_timeout_secs ,
120,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_check_interval_secs ,
0,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_check_count ,
32768 on i686 and 4194304 on x86-64, Linux's
.Dv PID_MAX_LIMIT ,
and
.Pa kernel/hung_task_warnings ,
10, as
.Xr lockup 7
describes;
.Pa kernel/hostname
and
.Pa kernel/domainname ,
the host and domain names of the reader's UTS namespace, as
.Xr uname 2
reports them, which root's write sets to the text before its first
newline, at most 64 bytes;
.Pa kernel/overflowuid
and
.Pa kernel/overflowgid ,
65534, the ids an id that a user namespace's map does not cover reads
as, which a write from 0 to 65535 sets;
.Pa kernel/randomize_va_space ,
whether
.Xr execve 2
places a new image at random addresses: 0 at fixed ones, any other
value with its stack, mmap base and a position-independent program
moved, and 2 or more with its heap moved as well; 2 on x86-64 and 0 on
i386, where a value written moves nothing; a write of any int sets it
for the next exec;
.Pa kernel/cap_last_cap ,
40, the highest capability Linux 6.12 numbers,
.Dv CAP_CHECKPOINT_RESTORE ,
which libcap reads to bound its sets;
.Pa kernel/panic ,
what a panic does once its record is out, as Linux's
.Va panic_timeout :
0 halts the machine; a number of seconds above 0 keeps the panic's
report on the screen that long, counting down, and then resets the
machine; one below 0 resets it at once.
.Ql panic= Ns Ar N
on the boot line sets it, 30 without, and a write of any int sets it;
.Pa kernel/panic_on_oops ,
1 with
.Ql oops=panic
on the boot line and 0 without, which a write of any int sets; an
exception the kernel takes in its own code panics whatever the value, as
Linux's oops does with it set;
.Pa kernel/sched_schedstats ,
0 at boot, whether the scheduler keeps the schedstats that
.Pa /proc/PID/sched
and
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug/sched/debug
show; a privileged write of 0 or 1 sets it, and any other value fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
.Pa kernel/sched_autogroup_enabled ,
1 at boot, whether the scheduler shares the processors between sessions
before the threads of each, as
.Xr sched 7
describes; a privileged write of 0 or 1 sets it, and any other value fails
with
.Er EINVAL .
Every thread keeps its session's group while it is 0, and the threads of
each group move to their group's queues or out of them at the write,
where Linux moves a thread only at its next change of group;
.Pa kernel/sched_rr_timeslice_ms ,
100, the slice of a
.Dv SCHED_RR
thread in milliseconds, counted in whole ticks of 4 ms rounded up; a
privileged write of any int sets it, and one at or below 0 restores 100;
.Pa kernel/sched_rt_period_us
and
.Pa kernel/sched_rt_runtime_us ,
1000000 and 950000, which a privileged write sets as Linux checks them:
the period from 1 to 2147483647 and the runtime from \-1, for none, to the
period, a write that would leave the runtime above the period failing with
.Er EINVAL ,
and one whose share of each processor, runtime over period, would fall
below the fair servers' with
.Er EBUSY ,
as Linux's
.Fn sched_dl_global_validate
refuses it.
As on a Linux built without real-time group scheduling, they throttle
nothing; the fair server keeps ordinary threads their share, as
.Xr sched 7
describes;
.Pa kernel/lock_stat ,
0 at boot, which a write of any int sets: while it is not 0 each hold of
a sleeping lock is timed for
.Pa /proc/fs/lockwait ,
as Linux's lock_stat times lock holds;
.Pa crypto/fips_enabled ,
0, since the kernel is not in FIPS mode, which libgcrypt and OpenSSL read;
.Pa user/max_mnt_namespaces ,
the mount namespaces each user may own at once in the reader's user
namespace and those below it, as Linux counts them: in the first, half the
threads Linux would allow the machine's memory, and in another 2147483647
until its root writes a value from 0 to 2147483647; past it
.Xr unshare 2
and
.Xr clone 2
fail with
.Er ENOSPC ;
.Pa kernel/random/boot_id ,
a random UUID drawn at its first read that stays for the boot, and
.Pa kernel/random/uuid ,
a new one at each read;
.Pa vm/max_map_count ,
65530, the regions of memory a process may map, as
.Xr mmap 2
counts them, which a write sets for the next mapping; a process already
past a value written keeps what it has;
.Pa vm/swappiness ,
60, and
.Pa vm/overcommit_memory ,
0, Linux's defaults, which nothing here acts on, there being no swap;
the settings of the kernel's write-back of dirty file data, at Linux's
defaults, which a privileged caller's write sets at once:
.Pa vm/dirty_expire_centisecs ,
3000, the hundredths of a second data stays dirty before the flusher
writes it back, and
.Pa vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs ,
500, the hundredths of a second between the flusher's turns, 0 for none
but those the background threshold starts, the first from 0 to
2147483647 and the second any int, a negative one taken as unsigned, as
Linux keeps it;
.Pa vm/dirty_background_ratio ,
10, and
.Pa vm/dirty_ratio ,
20, the per cent of the buffer cache's limit, the most memory dirty data
can hold, and of the slots of the page cache of a KRFFS root on a disk,
past which the flusher writes back and past which a writer writes back
itself, each from 0 to 100;
.Pa vm/dirty_background_bytes
and
.Pa vm/dirty_bytes ,
0, the same thresholds in bytes, which count in place of the ratio when
not 0: a write of a byte count, at least 1 and at least 8192, sets the
ratio to 0, and a write of a ratio sets the byte count to 0, as on Linux;
a new byte count that rounds up to more than 4294967295 pages, which only
x86-64 can write, fails with
.Er ERANGE
and leaves the setting as it was;
and
.Pa vm/dirtytime_expire_seconds ,
43200, which Linux reads for a mount with lazy times and nothing here
reads, since no mount keeps times lazily, from 0 to 2147483647.
The buffer cache's write-back goes without the disk's cache flush, while
that of the page cache of a KRFFS root on a disk writes each run through
the disk's durable write;
.Xr fsync 2 ,
.Xr sync 2 ,
an unmount and a shutdown flush it; and
.Pa net/ipv4/ip_forward ,
0, since the stack forwards nothing between interfaces: a privileged
caller's write of 0 succeeds, and of any other number fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
where Linux would begin to forward.
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_time ,
7200, and
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_intvl ,
75, the seconds before a TCP connection's first keepalive probe and between
probes, each from -8589934 to 8589934, INT_MAX over the tick rate of 250,
as Linux's
.Fn proc_dointvec_jiffies
takes them, 0 or less being due at once,
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_probes ,
9, the probes unanswered before the connection is reset, from 0 to 255,
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_retries2 ,
15, the retransmissions whose time an open connection is given up after,
from 0 to 255, and
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_mtu_probing ,
1, the mode of black-hole detection, from 0 to 255, a mode past 2 acting
as 2, are the settings of
.Xr tcp 7 ,
which a privileged caller's write sets for every connection at once.
.Pa net/core/netdev_max_backlog ,
1000, the datagrams the loopback backlog's input queue holds before it
drops the next, less one, as Linux's: a send to this machine past it
still succeeds and its datagram is lost, counted in
.Pa /proc/net/softnet_stat ;
a privileged caller's write of any int sets it, and a negative one drops
nothing, as Linux compares it unsigned.
A drain takes the whole input queue onto its process queue before it
delivers, so a reply sent while a datagram is delivered finds only what
waits behind it, and an echo is answered at a limit of 0.
.Pa net/core/netdev_budget ,
300, the datagrams a drain of the loopback backlog delivers before it
ends, in turns of 64, the last of which may pass it, and
.Pa net/core/netdev_budget_usecs ,
8000, two ticks, the microseconds after which a drain ends at the end of
its turn, timed by a stopwatch that reads the HPET, the PM timer, KVM's
clock or a trusted TSC and adds each turn's counts to the last, and so
runs on however long the drain holds interrupts off,
are Linux's; a drain that ends on either is
counted in
.Pa /proc/net/softnet_stat ,
and what it leaves is delivered at the sender's next return to user mode,
the idle wait or the tick.
A privileged caller's write of any int sets the first, and of two ticks
or more the second, as Linux 6.12.115 takes them.
.Pa net/ipv6/conf/ Ns Ar NAME ,
for
.Ar NAME
.Ql all ,
.Ql default
and each interface, holds IPv6's settings of that interface, which
.Ql default
gives an interface when it comes up, and which a privileged caller's
write sets at once:
.Pa accept_ra ,
1, 0 to ignore routers' advertisements, 2 to take them always and any
other value to take them unless forwarding;
.Pa autoconf ,
1, whether a router's prefix makes an address;
.Pa disable_ipv6 ,
0, which when not 0 takes every address of the interface away and stops its
IPv6, and set to 0 again starts it from its link-local address, making
its addresses again;
.Pa forwarding ,
0, which when not 0 makes the interface answer as a router and solicit none;
.Pa ra_defrtr_metric ,
1024, the metric of the default route each router's advertisement gives,
from 1 to 4294967295, which a write sets on the routes already learned as
well as on the next, where Linux waits for the next advertisement;
.Pa use_tempaddr ,
0, which above 0 makes a temporary address beside each made from a
router's prefix and at 2 prefers it as a source; and
.Pa stable_secret ,
an IPv6 address's text, the key from which the addresses made from a
router's prefix take their identifiers (RFC 7217), the interface's or,
when it has none,
.Ql default Ns 's ,
which reads as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits and fails with
.Er EIO
while none is written, and always for
.Ql all ;
a write changes that text from the file's offset, and the address it
then begins with becomes the secret, text that is no address failing with
.Er EIO ,
so that an empty write, or one past the text, sets the secret to the
text it reads, all zeros while none is written, as on Linux;
with none, the identifier is made from the station address.
Each but
.Pa ra_defrtr_metric
and
.Pa stable_secret
takes any int, as Linux's
.Fn proc_dointvec
does.
A write to
.Pa forwarding
or
.Pa disable_ipv6
whose number is refused returns its whole count and stores the value
the file had, as Linux's handlers of them do.
A write to
.Pa forwarding ,
.Pa disable_ipv6
or
.Pa use_tempaddr
of
.Ql all
writes it of every interface and of
.Ql default .
.Xr init 8
and
.Xr netup 8
write
.Ql default Ns 's
secret at start-up, from
.Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret .
Each file of
.Pa /proc/sys
is root's, with mode 0644 when it takes a write, 0600 for
.Pa stable_secret ,
0666 for
.Pa kernel/ns_last_pid
and 0444 otherwise.
An open, and each read and write after it, checks the caller as Linux's
.Fn sysctl_perm
does: an effective user ID of 0 has the owner's bits, an effective or
supplementary group ID of 0 the group's, and any other caller the
others'.
A holder of
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN
in the first user namespace has the owner's bits on the files of
.Pa net ,
and a holder of
.Dv CAP_SYS_RESOURCE
in the user namespace whose limits they are on those of
.Pa user ,
which other callers may only read; a descriptor of one reaches the
limits of the user namespace its open was made in, even after its
process enters another.
An open the mode refuses fails with
.Er EACCES ,
and a read or write through a descriptor whose process has since lost
the access with
.Er EPERM .
.Pa kernel/hostname ,
.Pa kernel/domainname ,
.Pa kernel/random/boot_id ,
.Pa kernel/random/uuid ,
.Pa kernel/ostype ,
.Pa kernel/osrelease ,
.Pa kernel/core_pattern
and
.Pa stable_secret
are strings, read on from any offset and written from it, as Linux's
.Fn proc_dostring
writes them: within the text the bytes written up to a newline replace
the rest, and past it a write changes nothing; every other file is a number,
which a read at an offset past 0 gives nothing of, as on Linux, so a
read after a write on one descriptor ends at once.
An empty write changes nothing and returns 0, and a write to a number at
an offset past 0 is taken whole and changes nothing, as Linux's
.Pa kernel/sysctl_writes_strict
of 1 has it.
Otherwise a number is read from the write's first 4095 bytes as Linux's
handler of the file reads it: spaces, then an optional minus, then a
digit that begins the number, octal after a 0 and hexadecimal after 0x,
of fewer than 21 characters with its sign, then a space, a tab, a
newline or the end, and the spaces after it.
A plus sign, any other byte after the number, a write of spaces alone
and a number out of the file's range, which is from 0 up unless this
entry says otherwise, fail with
.Er EINVAL .
A file of an int, read by Linux's
.Fn proc_dointvec ,
takes a minus, and a file of an unsigned long, read by
.Fn proc_doulongvec_minmax ,
.Pa fs/file-max ,
.Pa fs/pipe-user-pages-hard ,
.Pa fs/pipe-user-pages-soft ,
.Pa fs/inotify/max_user_instances ,
.Pa fs/inotify/max_user_watches ,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_timeout_secs ,
.Pa kernel/hung_task_check_interval_secs ,
.Pa user/max_mnt_namespaces ,
.Pa vm/dirty_background_bytes
and
.Pa vm/dirty_bytes ,
refuses one, -0 among them; each returns the bytes up to the end of the
spaces after the number, and every byte past the first 4095, and moves
the file's offset by them.
A file of an unsigned int, read by Linux's
.Fn proc_douintvec
or
.Fn proc_dou8vec_minmax ,
.Pa fs/nr_open ,
.Pa fs/pipe-max-size ,
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_probes ,
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_mtu_probing ,
.Pa net/ipv4/tcp_retries2
and
.Pa ra_defrtr_metric ,
refuses a minus and more than 4294967295, returns the write's whole
count and leaves the offset at 0, so a second write on one descriptor
sets the file again.
The writable files have mode 0644 but those named above, and an open of one for writing
by a caller without privilege fails with
.Er EACCES .
.It Pa /proc/stat
System counters, one named record per line.
The first line, and a line for each processor started, have this form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cpu  USER 0 SYSTEM IDLE 0 0 0 STEAL 0 0
cpu0 USER 0 SYSTEM IDLE 0 0 0 STEAL 0 0
.Ed
.Pp
The first four columns are user, nice, system, and idle time, in
hundredths of a second, Linux's USER_HZ, counted in millisecond units:
those that found the processor in user mode, none for nice, those that found it in
the kernel, an interrupt handler's included, and those that found it in an
idle wait.
The eighth is steal time: under KVM, the ticks of the time the host kept
the processor ready to run but ran something else, which are counted in
none of the others.
A processor counts elapsed time on its own clock since it started.
Time lost while its interrupts were held off is counted with the next
scheduler tick, as what that one found.
A stopped idle span is charged as idle and steal time once on wake; a
reader projects that span without changing the owning processor's counters.
The
.Li cpu
line is the processors' sums.
.Pp
The remaining lines have a name and one value:
.Bl -tag -width loopback_delivered_call
.It Li intr
Total interrupt count.
.It Li ctxt
Context switches since boot, on every processor, the idle task's among
them.
.It Li btime
Boot time as seconds since the epoch.
Setting the clock moves it by the size of the step.
.It Li processes
Processes created since boot.
.It Li process_pid_steps , process_uid_steps , process_timer_steps
Entries examined by the process pid index, real-user counts and active timer
list since boot, modulo 2^32.
Untimed blocked threads are absent from the timer list.
.It Li process_timer_passes
Passes over the active timer list since boot, modulo 2^32.
.It Li timer_passes_late
Timer passes since boot that a processor other than the boot processor made
because the boot processor's tick had not made one over two of its own
ticks, as when the boot processor runs with interrupts off, modulo 2^32.
.It Li tlb_shootdowns , tlb_shootdown_ipis
Requests since boot that had another processor drop user translations after
a change of page table entries, and the interprocessor interrupts they sent,
modulo 2^32.
A request covers one page, a range of up to 33 pages, or every user
translation, as Linux's
.Va tlb_single_page_flush_ceiling
has it; a page mapped where none was needs none.
.It Li scheduler_picks , scheduler_steps
Scheduler selections since boot, and the entries of the real-time threads'
ready lists they examined, modulo 2^32.
Blocked and ordinary threads add no entries to examine.
.It Li vm_gap_steps
Candidate gaps examined by the virtual memory free-range search since boot,
modulo 2^32.
.It Li clock_calls
System calls of the clock since boot, modulo 2^32:
.Fn clock_gettime ,
.Fn clock_gettime64 ,
.Fn gettimeofday
and
.Fn time .
A call the vDSO answers is not counted.
.It Li kvm_vms , kvm_vcpus , kvm_slots , kvm_tdp_pages , kvm_run_mappings
Live KVM virtual machines, virtual processors, memory slots, guest-physical
table pages and run-page mapping objects, on x86-64.
A fork shares a mapping object; the last reference releases it.
.It Li kvm_gpa_maps , kvm_gpa_map_pages
Maps of a guest's pages into a program of the host, and the frames
they hold, on x86-64: the blobs of a VM's memory that the shared-memory
GPU transport maps from the VM's own pages
.Pq Xr vgpu-shm 4 ,
counted until the driver frees the map, at the blob's free or the
device's reset or removal, after the VM's end too.
.It Li kvm_ioeventfd_signals , kvm_irqfd_raises
Guest writes that an ioeventfd took, and MSIs that an irqfd raised, since
boot, modulo 2^32, on x86-64: the work KVM did for a guest without an exit
to its caller.
These counts include objects retained by mappings after their descriptors close
.Pq Xr kvm 4 .
.It Li kvm_exits
Completed KVM backend entries since boot, including exits handled within
KVM_RUN, on x86-64.
Failed entries whose backend returns an error are excluded.
.It Li virtio_gpu_copied_bytes
Bytes that
.Xr virtio-gpu 4
has had its device copy between a resource and memory since boot, both
ways, counted once the device has answered the command: each 2D
transfer's rectangle at 4 bytes a pixel, and each 3D transfer's box by
whole blocks of its virgl format, whether a transfer ioctl or a
TRANSFER3D, COPY_TRANSFER3D or RESOURCE_INLINE_WRITE command of a virgl
submission carried it.
A box whose format has no block size, a planar or 3D ASTC one, or whose
resource the submission does not list counts nothing, and so does a
submission on a context of another capability set, Venus or a native
context.
A virgl submission whose list of resources the kernel cannot copy for
want of memory is still sent, and its commands count nothing.
.It Li window_free_min
The fewest pages the low window, the first 64 MiB, has had free at once
since boot: how near the kernel's allocations for device buffers came to
failing, however briefly.
.Pa /proc/meminfo Ns 's
.Li LowFree
is the count now.
.It Li vkms_vblank_overruns , vkms_vblank_skipped , vkms_crc_missed , vkms_crc_catchups
For the software display
.Xr vkms 4
since boot: the passes of its vblank timer that found more than one
frame gone, as Linux's
.Ql vblank timer overrun
warning counts them; the frames past the first those passes found,
which the vblank count takes in; the frames, of those, that got no CRC
while the CRC source was on, since no request was still pending to
cover them; and the composer's passes that entered the CRCs of more than
one frame at once, having fallen behind the vblanks.
All four are zero without the device.
.It Li vhost_net_detaches , vhost_net_detached_to_guest , vhost_net_detached_from_guest
Attachments of a tap to
.Xr vhost-net 4
that have ended since boot, and the frames they moved to and from the
guests, each attachment's added when the device lets its tap go and
counted modulo 2^32.
.It Li iommu_dma_faults , iommu_irq_faults
DMA and interrupt faults reported by VT-d since boot, on x86-64.
.It Li vfio_groups , vfio_devices , vfio_dma_mappings , vfio_dma_pages , vfio_dma_io_pages
Attached VFIO groups, referenced devices, DMA mappings, live pinned RAM pages
and mapped device pages, on x86-64.
Device pages do not count as locked RAM.
A container shared by groups is counted once.
.It Li msi_vectors_free
Free message vectors in the host's interrupt pool, on x86-64.
.It Li vdso_tsc
1 when the vDSO reads fine time from the TSC or KVM's clock page, and 0
when fine readings make a system call
.Pq Xr vdso 7 .
.It Li sleeps_interrupted
Sleeps in
.Xr nanosleep 2
and
.Xr clock_nanosleep 2
that a signal ended before their deadline, since boot.
A sleep that ran its whole length is not counted.
.It Li latency_timer_us
Longest gap between two timer passes, which advance the tick count and fire
the timeouts due, in microseconds.
.It Li bkl_wait_us , bkl_wait_max_us
Sum and longest acquisition of the big kernel lock, in microseconds since
boot, measured by the processor's time-stamp counter.
These include time when a hypervisor stopped a waiting processor.
They are zero when no invariant counter was calibrated.
.It Li bkl_wait_max_holds
Outer holds completed between the start of the longest acquisition and its
success, modulo 2^32, including a hold already in progress at its start.
The count belongs to the same acquisition as
.Li bkl_wait_max_us .
It counts releases, including an idle drop, rather than distinct processors.
.It Li bkl_wait_max_hold
The last 32 of those completed holds, or all when fewer, oldest first:
the entry, as for
.Li bkl_hold_max_entry ,
and the full hold time in microseconds, as for
.Li bkl_hold_max_us .
A hold already in progress when the acquisition began includes time before
the wait, so these times need not sum to the acquisition time.
.It Li bkl_hold_max_us
Longest hold of the big kernel lock, from its take to its release, in
microseconds since boot, measured by the same counter.
.It Li bkl_hold_max_entry
The kernel entry that made that hold, the one it counts under in
.Li bkl_hold_entry ,
printed as two numbers: its interrupt vector, 128 for a system call,
and its system call number, \-1 when the entry was no system call.
Where
.Li bkl_hold_entry
prints
.Li syscall 4
or
.Li vector 32 ,
this field prints
.Li 128 4
or
.Li 32 \-1 ;
a system call numbered 512 or above, which
.Li bkl_hold_entry
gathers under
.Li syscall -1 ,
prints its own number, or 512 when the hold was taken in such a call that
runs without the big lock.
Only
.Ql take 0
for a take after an idle wait and
.Ql take 1
for a thread's take after its switch read the same in both.
The switch lets the big lock go, and the thread takes it back when it runs
again, so a system call's hold after a sleep, a kernel thread's first hold
and its holds after a sleep are
.Ql take 1 .
Vector 0 with \-1 is the divide error or a hold begun in no entry other
than after a switch, such as a kernel thread's after it lets another
processor's claim go first.
.It Li bkl_hold_sample_us , bkl_hold_sample_pc
The oldest hold of the big kernel lock that an interrupt on the holding
processor has found, in microseconds since its take, and in hexadecimal the
kernel instruction that interrupt stopped, followed by up to seven return
addresses of its callers, read from the interrupted thread's kernel stack
alone.
A hold that keeps interrupts off is sampled only where it turns them on.
.It Li bkl_hold_max_mark
Five lines, one for each of 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds: the mark, then the
first such sample past it in the longest hold, its age in microseconds and
its instruction with callers; the age is 0 and the addresses absent when no
interrupt came that late.
.It Li bkl_hold_entry
Up to 16 lines, one for each kernel entry that held the big kernel lock
longest since boot, the longest first: the entry's kind,
.Li syscall
with its number,
.Li vector
with its interrupt vector, or
.Li take
with 0 for a take after an idle wait and 1 for a thread's take after its
switch, then its holds and their sum in microseconds.
A hold counts under the entry that took the lock, or under the entry the
thread was in when it took the lock in a call or an interrupt that runs
without it, up to the thread's next switch, idle wait or return to user
mode; a hold begun after a switch counts under
.Ql take 1
whatever entry the thread is in, vector 0 counts the holds begun in no
entry otherwise, as
.Li bkl_hold_max_entry
says, and
.Li syscall -1
gathers the system calls numbered 512 and above.
The boot processor's start-up hold is not counted here or in
.Li bkl_hold_max_us .
.It Li latency_disk_us
Longest block request from acceptance to completion, in microseconds.
.It Li latency_diskwait_us
Longest wait for a block request slot, in microseconds.
.It Li latency_console_us
Longest delay from a console line becoming readable to its being read,
in microseconds.
.It Li wlan_refused_tcp
TCP data segments a full wireless transmit queue refused, which TCP sends
again.
.It Li wlan_refused_ack
Bare TCP acknowledgements a full wireless transmit queue refused, which TCP
sends afresh on its next tick.
.It Li wlan_refused_other
Other frames a full wireless transmit queue refused, which are lost.
.It Li wlan_rx_held
Times a radio found the wireless receive queue full and left a frame in its
own buffers.
.It Li wlan_rx_wait_most
Longest time received frames waited for the wireless worker, in milliseconds.
.It Li wlan_tx_status_lost
Transmit completions the wireless data path had no room for.
Each frame's has a slot, so a nonzero count means a radio reported more than
it can hold.
.It Li wlan_rescue_key
Renewals of a pairwise key the station left because no key went in within 10
seconds of the access point's first message.
.It Li wlan_rescue_message4
Renewals the station left because message 4 failed under the old key and no
frame opened under the new key within 10 seconds.
.It Li wlan_rescue_silent
Renewals the station left because the access point acknowledged data under
the new key and did not pass on the gateway's answer to the ARP requests the
station sent within 10 seconds, and 2 seconds more for the last.
.It Li wlan_rescue_saved
Renewals that ended on the gateway's answer to an ARP request under the new
key, which proves that the access point passes the station's frames on.
.It Li wlan_rx_old_far
Frames dropped in the 2 seconds after a new pairwise key because their packet
numbers continue the old key's, and lie more than 256 from its last number:
a deep access point queue, sent under the new key with the old count.
.It Li loopback_dropped
Datagrams dropped because the loopback backlog was full, the sum of the
second column of
.Pa /proc/net/softnet_stat .
.It Li loopback_peak
Maximum number of datagrams in the loopback backlog at once.
.It Li loopback_delivered_tick
Datagrams delivered by the timer tick's loopback drain.
.It Li loopback_delivered_idle
Datagrams delivered by the idle wait's loopback drain.
.It Li loopback_delivered_call
Datagrams delivered by the loopback drain at system call exit, and at the
end of a thread, once its descriptors are closed.
.It Li loopback_left_idle
Datagrams a timer tick found on the loopback backlog while every processor was
halted in an idle wait.
The idle wait of a blocked process delivers the backlog before it halts, so
these are datagrams that waited for the timer with nothing running.
.It Li namecache_hits
Lookups of one name in one directory that the name cache answered, since
boot.
.It Li namecache_misses
Lookups the name cache passed to the file system, each a read of the
directory, since boot.
Only ext2's names are kept.
.It Li namecache_entries
Names the cache keeps now, at most 8192.
.It Li namecache_negative
Of those, the names recorded as absent.
.It Li ext2_node_visits
The inodes in memory that ext2's searches for an inode by its number have
read, since boot.
Each mount keeps them in a hash with a bucket for each, so a lookup the
name cache passes to ext2 reads one or two.
.It Li procs_running , procs_blocked
The processes runnable now, and those asleep in a wait no signal ends, in
the state
.Ql D ,
as Linux counts them.
.El
.It Pa /proc/uptime
Seconds since boot and the seconds the processors spent idle, summed over
them, separated by a space, each with two decimal places.
Both count millisecond clock units, independent of the scheduler's
interrupt rate: the first the global clock's, the second the idle units of
every processor, as the
.Li cpu
lines of
.Pa /proc/stat
count them.
The boot processor counts the ticks of a reference counter, the HPET, the
ACPI PM timer or a time-stamp counter that runs at one rate, so a tick
interrupt delivered late, or once for several that fell due while
interrupts were held off or the machine was stopped, counts every tick
that passed, and one delivered again counts none.
Both counts are kept in 64 bits.
.It Pa /proc/usb
One line per configured USB device:
.Dl port PATH SPEED VENDOR:PRODUCT class CLASS protocol PROTOCOL
.Ar PATH
is the root port followed by any hub ports.
.Ar SPEED
is
.Ql low ,
.Ql full ,
.Ql high ,
.Ql super ,
or
.Ql unknown .
Vendor and product IDs are hexadecimal; class and protocol are decimal.
A keyboard's or mouse's line ends with
.Ql reports N ,
the reports it has delivered since it was attached, so a program can count
what arrived however many reports the controller took in one interrupt.
.Pp
USB disks follow in this form:
.Dl diskN VENDOR PRODUCT SECTORS sectors K parts STATE flushes F
The sector count is 64-bit.
.Ar STATE
is
.Ql reading ,
.Ql ready ,
or
.Ql closing .
A disk not yet registered is named
.Ql disk? .
Write-protected disks append
.Ql ro ;
closing disks append
.Ql holds H .
After a USB disk has been present, a line gives free disk names
and disks reclaimed since boot:
.Dl disks FREE free RECLAIMED reclaimed
.Pp
While the stack has not finished with a device it has found, a last line
gives how many it has not finished with:
.Dl busy N
A device counts while it is being enumerated, recovered or closed, while a
hub is reading its descriptor, powering its ports or making its first scan
of them, and while a disk's units are being brought up.
The line goes once every device at a port is configured, refused, or waiting
for a controller slot or a free entry in the device table, so a program that waits for it to go and then reads the device
lines sees the end of enumeration.
A device a hub has not yet reported is not counted.
.Pp
The file is empty when no USB devices are configured, no disk
accounting remains and the stack is not busy.
.It Pa /proc/version
The kernel's name, release and version as
.Xr uname 2
gives them, in the form
.Ql "Linux version 6.18.0-rach2 #1 Rach2 1.0.0" ,
where Linux's line has the builder and the compiler after the release and
the build's date after the version, which this kernel does not record.
.It Pa /proc/PID
A process's world-readable and executable directories, mode 0555,
belong to its effective user and group IDs even when it is not dumpable.
Its other directories, files and links belong to those IDs while the
process is dumpable, and to root otherwise, as on Linux;
.Xr ps 1
and
.Xr top 1
read a process's user from the directory's owner.
A
.Xr stat 2
of a path works the owner out afresh, while a file, a link, a descriptor's
.Pa fdinfo
file, and the
.Pa fd ,
.Pa fdinfo
and
.Pa task
directories keep the owner the last lookup of their path gave them, as a
Linux inode does: an
.Xr fstat 2
of a descriptor open on one, an
.Dv O_PATH
one among them, reports the owner its open found, or a later
.Xr stat 2
of the same path, so a descriptor opened before
.Dv PR_SET_DUMPABLE
reports root until a path reaches the node again, and it keeps that owner
after the process exits or changes its IDs.
An
.Xr fchdir 2
into
.Pa fd
is judged by that owner.
Every such node a path reaches updates its retained owner, as Linux's
walk revalidates each component: one passed on the way, as
.Pa fd ,
.Pa fdinfo
or
.Pa task
is before the next component is looked up, even when that next component
is missing, and the last, so that
.Xr readlink 2
of a link, and a
.Xr stat 2
or
.Xr open 2
that follows it, update the link's owner.
A name relative to a directory descriptor or to the working directory is
walked from the root through the path of that directory, so it updates the
starting directory's retained owner too, where Linux starts the walk at
the directory and leaves its owner alone.
Listing
.Pa /dev
does not update the owner of
.Pa /proc/self/fd .
An
.Dv O_PATH | O_NOFOLLOW
descriptor on
.Pa exe ,
.Pa cwd ,
.Pa root ,
a descriptor link or a namespace link keeps the link's owner by the same
rule.
The process's directory and its
.Pa ns
directory work their owner out at each
.Xr fstat 2 .
The directories of
.Pa /proc
use the PID namespace of the process that mounted proc.
An inherited proc mount keeps its original numbering.
Mounting proc in a new PID namespace gives that namespace's numbering.
A descriptor of a process's fd or fdinfo directory keeps its target
across a change of namespace or a new mount over its pathname.
After the process is reaped, a working directory or descriptor on
.Pa /proc/PID/ns
still names the same directory.
A lookup of
.Pa \&..
there reaches its original parent.
A namespace name returns
.Er ENOENT .
The IDs in process status text use the reader's user and PID namespaces.
See
.Xr unshare 2 .
.It Pa /proc/PID/stat
One line of 52 space-separated fields, as Linux 6.12 writes it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
1 pid           2 (comm)         3 state         4 ppid
5 pgrp          6 session        7 tty_nr        8 tpgid
9 flags        10 minflt        11 cminflt      12 majflt
13 cmajflt     14 utime         15 stime        16 cutime
17 cstime      18 priority      19 nice         20 num_threads
21 itrealvalue 22 starttime     23 vsize        24 rss
25 rsslim      26 startcode     27 endcode      28 startstack
29 kstkesp     30 kstkeip       31 signal       32 blocked
33 sigignore   34 sigcatch      35 wchan        36 nswap
37 cnswap      38 exit_signal   39 processor    40 rt_priority
41 policy      42 blkio_ticks   43 guest_time   44 cguest_time
45 start_data  46 end_data      47 start_brk    48 arg_start
49 arg_end     50 env_start     51 env_end      52 exit_code
.Ed
.Pp
The command name is parenthesized; parentheses within it become
underscores.
State is
.Ql R
for running or ready,
.Ql S
for asleep in a wait a signal ends,
.Ql D
for asleep in one no signal ends, the disk's waits among them,
.Ql T
for stopped with its process by a stop signal,
.Ql t
for stopped by its tracer, or
.Ql Z
for zombie, as the
.Li State
line of
.Pa /proc/PID/status
says in words and as in Linux.
The parent, process group, and session IDs are recorded values.
Field 9 has Linux's
.Dv PF_KTHREAD ,
0x200000, for a kernel thread, and no other flag.
Fields 14 through 17 are the user and system time of all the process's
threads and its reaped children's, in 1/100 second ticks, as
.Xr times 2
reports them.
Fields 10 through 13 are the minor and major page faults counted the
same way, the process's then its reaped children's.
In
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/stat ,
fields 10, 12, 14 and 15 are the thread's own faults and time, as on Linux.
Field 19 is the nice value
.Xr setpriority 2
keeps, and field 18 the priority Linux reports: 20 more than the nice
value for an ordinary thread, \-101 for
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE ,
and \-1 less the effective real-time priority of a thread that has one,
its own under
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_RR
or one a priority-inheriting futex lends it.
Field 7 is the device number of the session's controlling terminal, 5:1
for the console and 136:N for
.Pa /dev/pts/N ,
and field 8 that terminal's foreground process group, which
.Xr ps 1
reads to list a terminal's processes; a session with no terminal has 0
and -1.
Field 20 is the number of threads, and field 21 is 0, as on Linux.
Field 22 is the time, in hundredths of a second, the process or thread was made
at, counted from boot, which
.Xr ps 1
subtracts from the uptime for the elapsed time; field 23 is the address
space's size in bytes and field 24 its pages in memory, as
.Pa /proc/PID/statm
gives them.
Field 25 is
.Dv RLIMIT_RSS ,
which the kernel does not keep, so it is the largest unsigned long, Linux's
default.
Fields 31 through 34 are the first 31 signals of the thread's own pending
set, its blocked set, and the signals the process ignores and catches.
Field 35 is 1 for a thread that is neither running nor ready, and for the
process's file only when the process has one thread.
Field 38 is the signal the process's end sends its parent,
.Dv SIGCHLD
or 0, and \-1 in a thread's file of any thread but the first.
Field 39 is the processor the thread last ran on, which
.Xr top 1
and
.Xr ps 1
show; fields 40 and 41 are its own real-time priority, without a
futex's donation, and its policy, without
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK .
Fields 26 through 28 and 45 through 51 are the bounds of the program's
code and data as its ELF headers place them, its first break, its stack
pointer at entry, and where its arguments and environment lie, and field 52
is the wait status of a thread that has ended.
A reader that may not trace the process, as
.Pa /proc/PID/mem
judges it, sees 1 in fields 26 and 27 and 0 in fields 28, 35, 45 through 51
and 52, as on Linux.
Fields 29, 30, 36, 37, 42, 43 and 44 are 0: Linux shows the stack and
instruction pointers only of a thread dumping core, counts no swap, has the
block I/O delay off by default, and counts guest time only for a KVM
virtual processor, which this kernel does not.
.It Pa /proc/PID/cmdline
The arguments as the program's initial stack has them, each ended by a
NUL; a process with no address space, as one that has ended has none,
shows its program name alone, where Linux shows nothing for one that has
ended.
A program whose last argument no longer ends in a NUL has set a title over
them, as
.Xr setproctitle 3
and Perl's
.Va $0
do, perhaps on into the environment strings that follow them; it shows,
as on Linux, the title alone: the first page from its first argument, up
to and with the first NUL.
At most the first 128 KiB of them are read, so a longer list ends inside
a string, without that string's NUL, and a page the address space cannot
read ends it where that page begins.
.It Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
The directory has mode 0500 and is owned by the process's effective IDs
while it is dumpable and by root otherwise.
Its size, as
.Xr stat 2
and
.Xr fstat 2
report it, is the number of descriptors the process has open, those an
open still under way has reserved among them, as a FIFO's open that waits
for its other end, 0 for a zombie, and an
.Xr fstat 2
or
.Xr statx 2
of a descriptor open on it fails with
.Er ENOENT
once the process is gone, as Linux's
.Fn proc_fd_getattr
does.
An open of it for reading, and a change of the working directory into it
by
.Xr chdir 2
or
.Fn fchdir ,
are judged by that mode and owner, whatever the caller's groups, with a
thread of the process always let in, as Linux's
.Fn proc_fd_permission
judges them; any other caller but a privileged one gets
.Er EACCES .
An open with
.Dv O_PATH ,
which reads nothing, succeeds for anyone, as Linux's does.
An
.Xr lstat 2
of a link in it takes only that check, as on Linux; reading a link, and
following it, are
.Er EACCES
as well to a caller that may not read
.Pa environ .
Each open descriptor N is a symbolic link owned as the directory is, whose
mode gives that owner read and execute when the descriptor reads, write and
execute when it writes, and no bit for an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor, as Linux's
.Fn tid_fd_update_inode
gives it.
Its target names what the descriptor is open on: a file by its path now,
from the reader's root, as Linux's
.Fn d_path
gives it, so that a rename of the file or of a directory above it moves
the link, with
.Ql " (deleted)"
once the file's last name is gone; a file of a type that keeps no names
by the path it was opened by; a device by its
.Pa /dev
path, a pipe or a FIFO as
.Ql pipe:[INODE] ,
a socket as
.Ql socket:[INODE] ,
a POSIX message queue as
.Ql / Ns Ar NAME ,
the path of its file in the
.Ql mqueue
file system, with
.Ql " (deleted)"
once
.Xr mq_unlink 2
has taken the name, an eventfd, epoll or timerfd descriptor as
.Ql anon_inode: Ns Ar NAME ,
and a file of
.Xr memfd_create 2
as
.Ql /memfd: Ns Ar NAME Ns Ql " (deleted)" ,
as Linux names each.
The directory lists the descriptors in the order of their numbers, and
the offset after an entry is its number plus 2, as on Linux, so that a
descriptor closed while the directory is read moves no other: a program
that reads the directory and closes each descriptor it finds before an
exec, as Chromium does, closes them all.
A followed link reports the descriptor's attributes.
An open of the link follows it: a file or a directory is opened again,
with the checks of an open by name and a description of its own, even
when it has lost its name; so is a device, whose driver opens it again,
so that a second DRM file of one card is a file of its own, after the
mode, owner and group of the node it was opened through are checked for
the access asked for: its entry in
.Pa /dev ,
or a device node of another file system, even one that has lost its
name, as Linux follows the descriptor's path; a pipe, a socket or a
terminal gets
the descriptor's own description, shared as
.Xr dup 2
shares it, when the descriptor has the access asked for, and the open is
.Er EACCES
otherwise, where Linux opens a pipe's other end anew.
Another process's descriptor needs a privileged caller or one with the
process's effective user ID.
An
.Xr execve 2
of the link runs the file the descriptor is open on, with the checks of
an exec by name, as on Linux.
.Pa /dev/fd/N ,
.Pa /dev/stdin ,
.Pa /dev/stdout
and
.Pa /dev/stderr
are the caller's links N, 0, 1 and 2, and
.Pa /dev/fd
is the caller's
.Pa fd
directory.
A descriptor that is closed has no link, also while its release waits
for a call of another thread that is using it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/cwd , /proc/PID/root
Symbolic links to the process's working directory, the path
.Xr getcwd 3
gives it, and to its root directory,
.Pa /
unless
.Xr chroot 2
moved it, for the path of the directory it moved it to.
A path that ends at either reaches the directory itself.
.Pp
A path that goes on past one of these links, or past a link under
.Pa fd ,
.Pa /dev/fd/N ,
.Pa /dev/stdin ,
.Pa /dev/stdout
or
.Pa /dev/stderr ,
as
.Pa /proc/PID/cwd/NAME
and
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N/NAME
do, goes on from the file or directory the link refers to, as on Linux,
and not from the text the link reads: from a directory wherever a rename
has put it, and from one removed while it is held, which has no names
left in it.
Every call that takes a path goes on so, the calls that make, rename and
remove names among them, and so does a
.Ql ..
after the link, which climbs from that directory.
A path past a descriptor open on a file is
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as a path past any file is, and so is one past a pipe, a socket or a
device; one past a descriptor that is not open is
.Er ENOENT .
A descriptor open on a directory of
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys ,
and a working directory there, go on from that directory's path.
A file opened through such a path has a link under
.Pa fd
that names it by the path of the directory the walk went through, not by
the link.
Reading one of these links, or going through one, needs what
.Pa exe
needs, and is
.Er EACCES
otherwise.
.It Pa /proc/PID/exe
A symbolic link to the program the process runs: the file its
.Xr execve 2
found, which the process keeps.
A path through it reaches that file itself, as on Linux, wherever a
rename has put it and after its name is removed, so an
.Xr open 2 ,
a
.Xr stat 2
or an
.Xr execve 2
of
.Pa /proc/self/exe
is one of the program itself, which is how a program runs itself again.
Reading the link gives the file's present path on the KRFFS root, and
for a file there whose name has been removed the path
.Xr execve 2
found it at followed by
.Ql " (deleted)" ,
as Linux names one.
On another file system it gives the path at which
.Xr execve 2
found the file, with each symbolic link in it replaced by its target, as
.Xr realpath 3
finds it and as Linux gives it.
The path is taken from the walk that found the file, so a link replaced
during the
.Xr execve 2
does not change it; a rename after the
.Xr execve 2
is not seen, where Linux gives the file's present path.
When the heap had no room for the copy of that path the
.Xr execve 2
keeps, the process has none: the link reads as an empty string on a file
system other than the KRFFS root, and as
.Ql " (deleted)"
alone for a file of the KRFFS root whose name has been removed.
Reading the link, or going through it, needs what
.Pa environ
needs past its mode.
An
.Xr lstat 2
of the link itself needs none of these, as on Linux; the link is absent
only while the process runs no program.
.It Pa /proc/PID/task
A directory with an entry for each thread of the process, named by its
thread ID, the thread whose ID is the process ID first.
Its link count is 2 and one for each live thread, as on Linux, where a
program that counts its threads reads it; the other directories here
have a count of 1.
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID
names nothing unless TID is one of the threads the directory lists, and
has the files of
.Pa /proc/TID ,
which are the thread's own where a thread has any, and
.Pa children
in place of a
.Pa task
directory of its own.
A descriptor open on it, or on its
.Pa fd ,
keeps both IDs: its link names that path, and a name relative to it is
found under it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/children
The process ID of each child of the process, as the reader's PID
namespace numbers it, each followed by one space, with no newline at the
end, in the order of the kernel's process table.
A child that has ended is listed until it is reaped, and one adopted
from a descendant, as
.Xr prctl 2
describes for a child subreaper, from its adoption.
The process has no such file of its own, as on Linux.
Linux lists in each thread's file the children that thread made; this
kernel records the process that made a child and not the thread, so the
file of the thread whose ID is the process ID lists every child of the
process, and the file of any other thread is empty.
.It Pa /proc/PID/comm
The program name and a newline.
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/comm
is the thread's own name, which
.Xr prctl 2 Ns 's
.Dv PR_SET_NAME
sets.
A write to either names the thread, as Linux takes it: its first 15
bytes, a newline among them kept, and the rest dropped.
Only a thread of the writer's own thread group may be named so; another
process's file is
.Er EINVAL
to write, as on Linux, and the open for writing is
.Er EACCES
to a caller that is neither privileged nor the process's owner.
A thread that has ended is
.Er ESRCH .
.It Pa /proc/PID/oom_score_adj
The adjustment Linux's out-of-memory killer adds to the process's score,
from \-1000 to 1000, in decimal and a newline, read as it is at each read
and not from a snapshot.
It is 0 at first, a child of
.Xr fork 2
takes its parent's,
.Xr execve 2
keeps it, and the threads of a process share it, so
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/oom_score_adj
is the process's.
This kernel has no out-of-memory killer and reads the value nowhere
else; it is kept for the programs that set it, as Chromium sets it for
its renderers.
.Pp
A write takes its first 12 bytes as one integer, as Linux reads them:
the bytes at either end that Linux's
.Fn isspace
takes for spaces are stripped, which are the space, tab, newline, vertical
tab, form feed, carriage return and 0xa0, and the number is decimal,
hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x
or octal after
.Ql 0 .
Text that is no integer is
.Er EINVAL ,
however many digits it begins with; an integer an int does not hold is
.Er ERANGE ,
and one it holds that is out of the range
.Er EINVAL .
A caller that is not privileged may not write a value below the floor,
the value the last privileged write left, 0 before any, and gets
.Er EACCES ;
a privileged caller may write any value, and its write sets the floor to
what it wrote, as a caller with Linux's
.Dv CAP_SYS_RESOURCE
does.
Opening the file for writing needs a privileged caller or its owner, the
owner of the process's directory, and
.Xr stat 2
reports it with mode 0644.
On Linux a write sets the value of every other process that shares the
address space too, a
.Xr vfork 2
child excepted; here it sets the one process's.
.It Pa /proc/PID/mounts
The text of
.Pa /proc/mounts ,
the reader's mounts, whatever process the file is of.
.It Pa /proc/PID/mountinfo
A line for each mount of
.Pa /proc/mounts ,
in its order, as util-linux's libmount reads it:
.Dl ID PARENT MAJOR:MINOR ROOT TARGET OPTIONS - TYPE SOURCE SUPER
The id is the mount's place in the table plus one; the root mount is 1
and its own parent.
.Ar MAJOR : Ns Ar MINOR
is the device
.Xr stat 2
gives the mount's files: 0:0 on a KRFFS root, 0:5 for
.Pa /dev ,
0:6 for
.Pa /proc ,
0:7 for
.Pa /sys ,
the numbers of the node of
.Pa /dev
a mount was made from, a partition or a loop device, and the place plus
16 for another mount; a bind mount has those of the mount it shows.
.Ar ROOT
is the root of the mount within its file system:
.Pa /
for a mount of a file system, and for a bind the path of its source
directory or file within the file system it shows.
.Ar OPTIONS
are the mount's own:
.Ql ro
only for a mount made read-only itself, by its mount or by
.Dv MS_REMOUNT
and
.Dv MS_RDONLY ,
otherwise
.Ql rw ,
then its flags from
.Ql nosuid
to
.Ql relatime .
The hyphen stands where Linux puts the propagation fields, which this
kernel has none of, and
.Ar SUPER
are the file system's:
.Ql ro
for a read-only file system, one mounted or remounted so, or turned
read-only by a failed write or a halt, otherwise
.Ql rw ,
then
.Ql sync ,
.Ql dirsync
and its other options, the same through every mount that shows it.
For example, a tmpfs reports its current nondefault size and inode
limits and its initial root mode, with the same values on its binds.
.It Pa /proc/PID/maps
A line for each region of the address space:
.Dl start-end perms offset major:minor inode path
with the rights as
.Ql r ,
.Ql w ,
.Ql x
or
.Ql - ,
then
.Ql p
for a private region or
.Ql s
for a shared one, and from column 73 the file the region maps,
.Ql [heap] ,
.Ql [stack] ,
.Ql [vvar]
and
.Ql [vdso]
for the vDSO's data page with KVM's clock page and its library on x86-64,
both private, the first never writable and the library copied on a write
.Pq Xr vdso 7 ,
the program for the segments of its image, or the dynamic linker the
program names for the segments of the linker's, but not for the anonymous
pages of a segment's bss past its file's, which have no name, as on Linux,
by the present path of the
file exec opened, with
.Ql " (deleted)"
once its name is gone, as
.Pa exe
reads, and
.Ql "/dev/zero (deleted)"
for shared anonymous memory, as on Linux.
The offset is the file's at the region's first page, and 0 for a region
that maps no file.
The device's major and minor numbers, in hexadecimal, and the inode, in
decimal, are those
.Xr stat 2
gives the file, and read
.Ql 00:00 0
for a region of no file; a segment of the program or of the linker that
was loaded and not mapped, as the debug shell loads its program, has those
of the file it names, and shared anonymous memory has the device of the
internal tmpfs mount that holds memfds and System V segments and an inode
number of its own there, as Linux gives it those of its shmem file.
A region with no path ends at the space after the inode, as on Linux.
A mapped file's path is the target of its
.Pa fd
link, built at each reading of
.Pa maps ,
.Pa smaps
or
.Pa numa_maps
from the reader's root, as Linux's
.Fn d_path
builds it.
A device, a DRM buffer, a dma-buf or a PCI resource file of
.Pa /sys ,
which maps itself, is named by its
.Pa fd
link's target when it was mapped:
.Pa /dev/ Ns Ar name ,
.Ql anon_inode: Ns Ar name
or the file's path in
.Pa /sys ,
which is the same from every root.
It and
.Pa smaps
need what
.Pa environ
needs, and are
.Er EACCES
to another caller.
.It Pa /proc/PID/smaps
Each region's
.Pa maps
line, followed by lines of a name, a colon and a size in kilobytes, as
Linux writes them: Size, KernelPageSize and MMUPageSize, Rss, Pss,
Shared_Clean, Shared_Dirty, Private_Clean, Private_Dirty, Referenced,
Anonymous, AnonHugePages, the memory transparent huge pages map in the
region, the huge zero page's aside, Swap, SwapPss and Locked; a
THPeligible line, 1 when a fault may map a huge page in the region and 0
otherwise, a plain number; where the protection keys are on, a
ProtectionKey line with the region's key, a plain number
.Pq see Xr pkeys 7 ;
and a VmFlags line of
.Ql rd ,
.Ql wr ,
.Ql ex ,
.Ql sh
for a shared region that may be made writable,
.Ql mr ,
.Ql mw
unless the region may not be made writable,
.Ql me
unless the region maps
.Xr memfd_secret 2 ,
.Ql ms
for every shared region, one of a file opened without write among them,
.Ql gd ,
.Ql pf
for a mapping of a device object,
.Ql lo
for a locked region,
.Ql io ,
again for a mapping of a device object,
.Ql dc
for a range
.Xr madvise 2 Ns 's
.Dv MADV_DONTFORK
marked,
.Ql de
for a mapping of a device object, which cannot grow,
.Ql lf
for a region locked on fault
.Pq see Xr mlock 2 ,
.Ql ac
for one counted as committed memory: the stack, the heap, a writable
segment of the program, and a private mapping that was writable when
.Xr mmap 2
made it or that
.Xr mprotect 2
first made writable, even after it stops being writable, except anonymous
memory of which no page was written while it, or the region it was split
from, held it, which
.Xr mprotect 2
uncounts when it takes write away and changes the protection,
.Ql nr
for a mapping made with
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE ,
which is never counted,
.Ql wf
for a range
.Dv MADV_WIPEONFORK
marked,
.Ql dd
for a mapping of a device object or
.Xr memfd_secret 2 ,
which a core file leaves out,
.Ql hg
and
.Ql nh
for a range
.Dv MADV_HUGEPAGE
and
.Dv MADV_NOHUGEPAGE
marked, and
.Ql sl
for a region
.Xr mseal 2
sealed, in that order, the order of Linux's flag bits.
The vDSO's pages have the flags Linux's special mappings have:
.Ql mr mw me de
for [vdso] and
.Ql mr pf io de dd
for [vvar], after their access.
Rss counts the region's pages in memory, clean or dirty by the page
table's dirty bit and shared only in a shared region.
Anonymous counts every page of a private region of no file, and each
page of a private file region once a write, or a private copy of the
page, has made its frame anonymous; gdb's gcore leaves a file region
with no such page out of a core.
The frame keeps this classification across fork and copy-on-write,
independently of the page-table dirty bit.
The kernel does not count the processes that map a page, so Pss is Rss.
Swap and SwapPss read 0, and Locked is Rss for a locked region and 0
for another.
BusyBox's
.Xr pmap 1
and smemcap read it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/numa_maps
A line for each region, as Linux writes it on a machine of one memory
node: the region's start in hexadecimal; the memory policy that governs
it; what it maps, which is
.Ql file=
and the path
.Pa maps
gives it, with a newline, a tab, a space and
.Ql =
written in octal, as
.Ql \e012 ,
.Ql \e011 ,
.Ql \e040
and
.Ql \e075 ,
or
.Ql heap
or
.Ql stack ,
or nothing for anonymous memory; and, for a region with pages of memory,
their counts:
.Ql anon=
the anonymous ones,
.Ql dirty=
those written,
.Ql mapped=
all of them when they are neither all anonymous nor all written,
.Ql N0=
those on node 0, which is all of them, and
.Ql kernelpagesize_kB=4 .
The policy is the one
.Xr mbind 2
gave the region's first page, written as Linux names it:
.Ql default ,
.Ql prefer ,
.Ql bind ,
.Ql interleave ,
.Ql local ,
.Ql prefer (many)
or
.Ql weighted interleave ,
then
.Ql =static ,
.Ql =relative
or
.Ql =balancing
for a mode flag, and
.Ql :0
for a policy with nodes.
A region
.Xr mbind 2
gave no policy shows the policy
.Xr set_mempolicy 2
gave the thread read.
A page that was only read is Linux's zero page and is not counted, nor is
device memory.
Linux's
.Ql mapmax=
and
.Ql active=
are not written, since the kernel counts no mappings of a page and keeps
no active list.
The file needs what
.Pa maps
needs.
numastat reads it.
.It Pa /proc/PID/status
Lines of a name, a colon, a tab and a value:
Name, Umask, State, Tgid, Pid, PPid, TracerPid, Uid and Gid (real,
effective, saved and file-system IDs), FDSize, Groups, the memory sizes
VmPeak to VmSwap in kilobytes, of which VmLck is the size of the regions
locked by
.Xr mlock 2
and VmPin the pages drivers pinned for devices and charged to the
process, VmHWM the most the process has had resident, and RssAnon,
RssFile and RssShmem the parts of VmRSS that are the process's own
pages, a file's, and those of a file in memory or of shared anonymous
memory, Threads, the signal sets SigPnd, ShdPnd,
SigBlk, SigIgn and SigCgt in hexadecimal, the capability sets,
NoNewPrivs, Seccomp and Seccomp_filters, the thread's seccomp mode and
the number of its filters
.Pq see Xr seccomp 2 ,
NStgid, NSpid, NSpgid and NSsid, the IDs in each PID namespace from
the reader's down to the process's own, separated by tabs,
Cpus_allowed and Cpus_allowed_list, the processors the thread may run
on as
.Xr sched_getaffinity 2
reports them, as a mask in words of 32 bits and as a list of ranges, and
voluntary_ctxt_switches and nonvoluntary_ctxt_switches, the times the
scheduler switched away from the thread while it slept or was stopped,
and while it could still run.
.Pp
Rach2 also reports, for every thread, TimedWakeCount, the thread's
completed timed wakes, and TimedWakeLast, TimedWakeWorst and
TimedWakeSum, the last wake, the wake with the longest total delay, and
the sums of all completed wakes.
A wake is completed at the thread's return to user mode, so a kernel
thread's lines stay 0.
Each of the latter three has eight microsecond columns:
expiry to the servicing timer interrupt, that interrupt's wait for the
big kernel lock after expiry, remaining timer work, run-queue wait,
running toward the return to user mode, blocked again before that return,
the queued CPU's waits for the big lock, and the returning thread's waits
for the big lock.
The last two are subsets of the queue and return columns.
Only the first six are added for the total.
The record ends just before the final register restore and return to
user mode, and begins at the rounded deadline tick, after the wait is armed.
The count and individual times wrap at 2^32; sums have 64 bits.
A stage must be shorter than about 35 minutes.
These fields start empty in a new thread and also appear in its
.Pa /proc/PID/task/TID/status .
.Pp
CapPrm and CapEff are the capabilities the thread holds, those it has not
given up when its effective user ID is 0, and none otherwise, with the
capabilities of file access in CapEff by the file-system user ID
.Pq see Xr capget 2 and Xr setfsuid 2 ;
CapBnd is the bounding set, which
.Dv PR_CAPBSET_DROP
of
.Xr prctl 2
narrows; CapInh and CapAmb are the inheritable and ambient sets that
.Xr capget 2
and
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT
of
.Xr prctl 2
keep.
.It Pa /proc/PID/statm
Seven sizes in pages: total, resident, shared, text, library, data with
stack, and dirty; the third, fifth and seventh read 0.
.It Pa /proc/PID/auxv
The auxiliary vector the program was given, as pairs of 32-bit words
through the
.Dv AT_NULL
pair.
.It Pa /proc/PID/limits
Linux's table of the sixteen resource limits, a line of column names and
a line for each: its name, its soft and hard values or
.Ql unlimited ,
and its unit, in Linux's columns.
The six the kernel keeps, the file size, the stack, the core file, the
processes, the open files and the address space, have the process's
values, and the others are unlimited, as
.Xr prlimit64 2
reports them.
.It Pa /proc/PID/syscall
The system call the thread is in, in Linux's form: its number, its six
arguments, and the user stack and instruction pointers, all but the number
in hexadecimal with
.Ql 0x ;
the number and the two pointers alone when the number is negative, and
.Ql -1
and the two pointers when the thread entered the kernel by a fault or an
interrupt;
.Ql running
when it is ready or on a processor;
.Ql 0
with every other field
.Ql 0x0
for a sleeping kernel thread; and
.Ql -1 0x0 0x0
for an ended leader.
The number is the one a tracer left, and an i386 call on x86-64 has i386's
number and arguments.
The text is made at the first
.Xr read 2 ,
so the caller's own thread shows that read.
The file has mode 0400, which an open checks as for
.Pa environ ,
and is
.Er EACCES
to a caller that is not its owner; a read by a caller that lacks what
.Pa environ
needs past its mode is
.Er EPERM ,
as on Linux.
.It Pa /proc/PID/wchan
The name of the kernel function the thread sleeps in, with no newline, or
.Ql 0
when it runs, is ready to, has ended, or the caller lacks what
.Pa environ
needs past its mode.
A wait names itself with Linux's function where Linux has one:
.Ql pipe_read ,
.Ql pipe_write ,
.Ql pipe_wait_writable
for
.Xr vmsplice 2 ,
.Ql wait_for_partner ,
.Ql futex_wait_queue ,
.Ql futex_wait_multiple ,
.Ql futex_lock_pi ,
.Ql do_wait ,
.Ql wait_for_vfork_done ,
.Ql de_thread ,
.Ql hrtimer_nanosleep ,
.Ql do_cpu_nanosleep ,
for a sleep resumed after a stop
.Ql do_syscall_64 ,
or
.Ql do_int80_emulation
for an i386 program, on the x86-64 kernel and
.Ql __do_sys_restart_syscall
on the i686 kernel,
.Ql syslog_print ,
.Ql do_sys_poll ,
.Ql do_select ,
.Ql ep_poll ,
.Ql sigsuspend ,
.Ql do_sigtimedwait ,
.Ql __do_sys_pause ,
and
.Ql do_signal_stop
or
.Ql ptrace_stop
for a stopped thread.
A thread in
.Xr pause 2
reads
.Ql __do_sys_pause
whichever ABI made the call: Linux's entries of both ABIs are aliases of
that function, and its symbol table lists it first of the three.
Any other wait reads as Rach2's
.Ql wq_wait_event ,
.Ql wq_wait_event_nointr
or
.Ql wq_wait_event_idle .
.It Pa /proc/PID/autogroup
The process's session group, as Linux's autogroup shows it:
.Dq Li /autogroup-ID nice N ,
with the group's number, given from 1 in order of creation, and its nice
value, which weighs the group against the others as a thread's weighs it
against its peers; nothing for a process in no group, as the processes
that never called
.Xr setsid 2
are.
The file has mode 0644 and is the process's alone, with no copy under
.Pa task .
A write of an int from \-20 to 19, read with the white space around it
taken off and in C's bases, sets the group's nice value, as on Linux.
A lower value than the writer's
.Dv RLIMIT_NICE
allows fails with
.Er EPERM ,
a value out of range with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a write by a caller without privilege within 100 ms of the last
change by anyone with
.Er EAGAIN ;
a write for a process in no group changes nothing and succeeds.
.It Pa /proc/PID/schedstat
Three numbers on one line, as Linux prints the thread's scheduler
statistics: its time on a processor in nanoseconds, the run time the
scheduler charges it, which
.Ql se.sum_exec_runtime
in
.Pa /proc/PID/sched
shows; the nanoseconds it waited ready on a
run queue for a processor, to the microsecond; and the times it was
switched onto a processor.
The file of a process is its leader's, and the file under
.Pa task
a thread's own.
.Xr kvm 4
adds the second number's growth to a guest's steal time.
.It Pa /proc/PID/sched
The thread's scheduler state, as Linux 6.12 prints it: a header line of
its name, its id in the reader's PID namespace and its process's thread
count, a line of dashes, then one line for each value, its name in 45
places and a colon.
A count fills the 21 places after the colon; a time is milliseconds with
six decimals, the whole milliseconds in 14 places, rounded toward zero, so
that a time above \-1 ms shows no sign.
The lines are
.Ql se.exec_start ,
.Ql se.vruntime ,
.Ql se.sum_exec_runtime
and
.Ql se.nr_migrations ;
while
.Pa kernel/sched_schedstats
is 1, the 28 schedstats of Linux's
.Vt struct sched_statistics ,
from
.Ql sum_sleep_runtime
to
.Ql nr_wakeups_idle ,
then
.Ql avg_atom ,
the run time per switch, and
.Ql avg_per_cpu ,
the run time per migration, each \-1 for none;
.Ql nr_switches ,
.Ql nr_voluntary_switches
and
.Ql nr_involuntary_switches ;
.Ql se.load.weight
and the seven
.Ql se.avg.*
load averages;
.Ql policy
and
.Ql prio ,
the priority of field 18 of
.Pa stat
plus 100;
.Ql se.slice ,
in nanoseconds, for an ordinary, batch or idle thread, or
.Ql dl.runtime
and
.Ql dl.deadline
for
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE ;
and
.Ql clock-delta ,
the nanoseconds between two readings of the clock.
Linux's NUMA balancing lines are left out, since Rach2 balances no NUMA
memory.
The file is mode 0644: its owner's write of anything clears the thread's
schedstats, as on Linux.
.It Pa /proc/PID/fdinfo
A file for each open descriptor, named by its number, beginning with two lines:
.Ql pos: ,
the offset, and
.Ql flags: ,
in octal, the access mode with
.Dv O_APPEND ,
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
.Dv O_PATH
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
Then come two more, as on Linux:
.Ql mnt_id: ,
the id of the descriptor's mount, which
.Pa /proc/PID/mountinfo
gives it and
.Xr stat 2 Ns 's
.Fn statx
reports as
.Dv STATX_MNT_ID ,
and
.Ql ino: ,
the inode number
.Xr fstat 2
reports.
A file of
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys
has the id of that file system's mount.
The descriptor keeps the id of the mount it was opened through, even when
another mount now covers its path.
For a dma-buf with a name set by
.Dv DMA_BUF_SET_NAME ,
.Ql name:
gives that name.
On x86-64, a KVM VM's descriptor,
.Ql anon_inode:kvm-vm ,
adds
.Ql kvm_irqchip: ,
the interrupt controller in the kernel,
.Ql kernel ,
.Ql split
or
.Ql none ,
then a
.Ql kvm_ioapic_pin:
line for each pin of the kernel's I/O APIC with a count, the pin and its
inputs raised, coalesced into an interrupt still pending, held by the
entry's mask or remote IRR, delivered, and delivered to no APIC, and a
.Ql kvm_vcpu_vector:
line for each vector of each vCPU with a count, the vCPU's index, the
vector and its interrupts accepted into IRR, dropped by a disabled APIC,
acknowledged into ISR, injected at an entry, saved from an exit that cut
their delivery off, and ended by an EOI; one taken from the PIC is ended
at the PIC and counts no EOI.
The counts are Rach2's own, not Linux's, and wrap at 2^32.
Linux adds lines of each kind of file.
It is
.Er EACCES
to a caller that may not read
.Pa environ ,
and a change of the working directory into it through a descriptor opened
before the process ended is
.Er ESRCH .
.Xr chroot 2
to it gives the caller a root that holds nothing, the empty root of a
file system no path reaches.
.It Pa /proc/PID/ns
A directory of mode 0511, owned by the process's effective IDs while it
is dumpable and by root otherwise, which an open for reading checks as
the mode says, so that a caller other than its owner and root gets
.Er EACCES ,
the process itself among them once it is not dumpable;
.Dv O_PATH
opens it for anyone.
It has six links,
.Pa user ,
.Pa pid ,
.Pa mnt ,
.Pa net ,
.Pa uts
and
.Pa ipc ,
to the process's namespaces, which a listing of the directory gives,
each named
.Ql KIND:[INODE] ,
the inode number of the namespace; two processes share a namespace when
the names are the same.
An open of a link gives a descriptor for
.Xr setns 2 .
The first namespaces have Linux's inode numbers for the user, PID, UTS
and IPC kinds.
.It Pa /proc/PID/uid_map , /proc/PID/gid_map
The ID maps of the process's user namespace, a line for each extent: the
first ID in the namespace, the first ID it maps to as the reader's
namespace sees it, or as the namespace's parent sees it for a reader in
the namespace, and the count, each right-aligned in ten columns.
The first namespace reads
.Ql "0 0 4294967295" .
A map is written once, in one write of fewer than 4096 bytes and at most
340 lines, as on Linux since 4.15, by a process of the namespace or its
parent; a 341st line or a write of a page or more fails with
.Er EINVAL .
A map of more than five extents reads back sorted by the first ID, as
Linux lists one.
A writer privileged in the parent maps IDs the parent maps, each extent's
within one extent of the parent's map, and another writer maps one ID,
its own effective ID, and for the groups only after
.Pa setgroups
was set to
.Ql deny .
.It Pa /proc/PID/setgroups
.Ql allow
or
.Ql deny ;
a namespace whose group map is not yet written takes
.Ql deny ,
after which
.Xr setgroups 2
fails with
.Er EPERM
in it and below it.
A namespace below one that denies it reads
.Ql deny
from the start, and its group map may then be written by a process
without privilege in the parent, as Linux allows.
.It Pa /proc/PID/environ
The environment strings of the initial stack, each ended by a NUL, at
most their first 128 KiB, cut as
.Pa cmdline
is.
It and
.Pa auxv
have mode 0400: only the owner, or a caller with privilege over files, may
open them.
Past the mode, they, the
.Pa exe
link and the other files that say so need what Linux's ptrace check with
.Dv PTRACE_MODE_FSCREDS
needs: a thread of the process, a privileged caller, one whose effective
user ID is 0 and which kept its capabilities
.Pq see Xr capget 2 ,
or one whose file-system user and group IDs are each of the process's
three user and group IDs, while the process is dumpable and not
privileged.
.It Pa /proc/PID/mem
The process's memory, read and written at the descriptor's offset, which
is the address, as GDB uses it through
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2 .
An address of no region, of a guard region, or in the vDSO's data pages,
.Li [vvar] ,
is
.Er EIO .
The access is forced, as a tracer's is and as on Linux: a
.Dv PROT_NONE
region is read, an untouched page of private anonymous memory as zeros,
and a write reaches a private one and read-only program text, though not a
read-only or
.Dv PROT_NONE
shared one.
A transfer that runs into such an address returns the bytes it did
before it, as on Linux, and moves the offset past them.
The file has mode 0600, and opening it needs the owner's access or
privilege over files, and then what
.Pa environ
needs.
The descriptor reaches the address space the open found while anything
runs in it, a thread of the process or another process that shares it
with
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
as on Linux: once the last of them has run another program with
.Xr execve 2
or ended, before its parent reaps it, a read or a write returns 0, so a
program the process executes
later, a set-user-ID one among them, is not reached through a descriptor
opened before.
The descriptor keeps none of the memory, which goes when the last of them
does.
.It Pa /proc/self
The calling thread's process.
.Pa /proc
lists each process once; the ID of a thread that did not begin its
process also names a directory, which is not listed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr memory 7 ,
.Xr rach2 7 ,
.Xr tcp 7 ,
.Xr vdso 7
